WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:27.939
Where are your placements?

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:29.669
All Benhaven?

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:31.650
And Chapel Haven. Ok, good. So. 

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:36.830
You're going to see some kids and adults today
on some of our video clips

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:40.490
that I will probably remind you of some people
you've seen, not because they are the people you've seen,

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.630
but because the types of issues and behaviors
demonstrating are

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.070
relatively consistent. 

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.550
And, 

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.439
I'll give you a little caveat about

00:00:50.439 --> 00:00:54.250
what we're going to do for the next hour and
a half or so. 

00:00:54.250 --> 00:01:02.680
How do you guys handle questions? Whenever
they want, good?É Ok. 

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.930
I have two ground rules, if you don't mind.
The first one is if you have a question, don't

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.460
wait,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.079
ok? Just ask it. 

00:01:08.079 --> 00:01:11.100
Because ten other people in this room have the
same question and they're shy. 

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.340
The second thing is,

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.750
I know you're doing some practicums, so you've got some
other clients you're working with.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.590
If you want to ask a question about one of
the people you're working with,

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.210
I don't mind that, but let's save it till the whole
thing is over and I'll stick around for a little

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.280
bit, ok?

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.990
I imagine you've got supervisors on the sites
where you are, so you wouldn't

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.570
necessarily want to come to me with that 'cause I
don't know your clients,

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.500
but if you had something like that. If you 
wanna ask a question about

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.040
somebody on the videotapes,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.490
I can certainly respond to those.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:42.340
If it looks like we're running a little bit
later on some of the discussions and everything

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:47.020
like that, then what I'm going to do is probably
hold a couple of videotapes, but I'll show 'em to you as soon as we finish.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.479
Ok? 'Cause they're kind of fun.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:52.310
And what I want to do is use the videotapes
as a way of giving you an example of what

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.750
we're talking about.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.830
We're going to spend pretty much
all of this time talking about

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.930
what happens when you've got behavioral issues
with people with autism, and

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.119
you may have heard this from some of the other
presenters, but

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:09.419
the most important thing I can tell you today,
and it's really the take-home message, is: behavior is communication,

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:13.269
and you're going to spend 85% of your time on
the assessment side,

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:15.349
ok? Because with a good assessment,

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:20.169
you will very clearly inform your intervention
practice, and with a poor assessment,

00:02:20.169 --> 00:02:21.669
you may get lucky,

00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:24.160
or you may be completely off base and you
may end up

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.669
either encouraging or reinforcing a behavior
you really don't want to see. Or

00:02:28.669 --> 00:02:30.289
you might end up doing some harm. 

00:02:30.289 --> 00:02:32.330
And when I say harm, we're talking about 

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.529
problems in learning, problems in behavior, and everything
that goes beyond that.

00:02:36.529 --> 00:02:45.019
And...

00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:48.179
We're gonna start with a little story, and

00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:49.549
this is 

00:02:49.549 --> 00:02:52.619
about a kid that I worked with a number
of years ago.

00:02:52.619 --> 00:02:57.329
I was called into a school system-- not up here
in Connecticut, it's when I worked down in DC-- and I was

00:02:57.329 --> 00:03:00.209
asked to do some consulting around this kid. 

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:04.039
The kid had a pretty important and problematic
behavior, and

00:03:04.039 --> 00:03:06.110
it kind of went like this. He'd be--

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.439
it was in a regular classroom, it was in a public school--

00:03:08.439 --> 00:03:13.249
it's a guy with autism, he was about nine years old--
got language, he reads, does some academics.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:17.259
In a learning center, which is more or
less a special education environment with

00:03:17.259 --> 00:03:24.259
some integration into the mainstream at various
points during the day. Now, this

00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:28.900
request for consultation is around significant
challenging behavior, and it goes like this:

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.369
the guy is 

00:03:30.369 --> 00:03:31.619
sitting at a table--

00:03:31.619 --> 00:03:33.810
maybe like one of these, about half that size--

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.609
four students sitting around the table, 

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:37.519
they're all doing their seat work.

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:38.540
Well, in the middle

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.050
of something,

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.419
our fellow here stands up,

00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:43.899
grabs a-hold of the table,

00:03:43.899 --> 00:03:47.209
screams "The Rosenberg findout!" and flips the table.

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:51.889
Everything scatters. Tips the table
on top of the other kids. 

00:03:51.889 --> 00:03:53.929
So the 

00:03:53.929 --> 00:03:55.329
person who seems-- who

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:58.540
I'm working with, we're looking at this and
we're thinking, "Alright, well,

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.869
what's the best way to think about this problem?"

00:04:00.869 --> 00:04:04.709
The guy's name isn't Rosenberg, it has nothing
to do with Rosenbergs.

00:04:04.709 --> 00:04:07.240
It has to do with something else, and we

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.239
have the opportunity to try to sort that out.

00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:14.659
We go through a process, and the process is
really one that I'm going to outline for you this

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:17.609
afternoon, including the treatment side of it. 

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:22.629
But the process is one of investigation. It's
one of asking "why" questions. Why would he do this?

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.129
Under what circumstance?

00:04:24.129 --> 00:04:25.550
What precipitated it?

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.639
What was the--

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:30.049
what we would call the setting events and the antecedent
conditions?

00:04:30.049 --> 00:04:33.699
And when we think about it this way, it allows
us to ask questions,

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:35.030
pose responses

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.460
based on observable facts or data.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.979
Those are the things that we as behavioral
analysts and psychologists who are working in

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:42.780
this field

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.270
sort of live and die by, this-- when you
work with significant challenging behavior,

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:47.120
with people

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.199
with developmental disabilities.

00:04:49.199 --> 00:04:53.830
I'm going to use autism today as an example,
but you could have an individual who's got

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:58.059
cri du chat syndrom or somebody who's
got an intellectual disability by virtue

00:04:58.059 --> 00:04:59.539
of Down syndrome--

00:04:59.539 --> 00:05:01.400
any number of other situations.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.399
You're going to see some videos of kids who
are severely, profoundly typical.

00:05:05.399 --> 00:05:06.550
Who are just

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.860
typically developing toddlers-- to

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:13.319
to give you an illustration of some important
points.

00:05:13.319 --> 00:05:16.039
The good part of this is that behavior

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:17.240
has

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:17.819
relatively--

00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:19.930
in many cases, relatively clear-cut

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.349
contingencies.

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:22.350
What starts it,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.999
what keeps it going. 

00:05:23.999 --> 00:05:28.039
And our job as folks trying to solve these
issues is really to wrap them up and understand

00:05:28.039 --> 00:05:32.439
what's going on so we can help the kids or
adults learn to respond or behave in a somewhat

00:05:32.439 --> 00:05:34.909
different way that's going to be a little more socially appropriate
than

00:05:34.909 --> 00:05:37.509
flipping tables, screaming "The Rosenberg Findout."

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:40.909
"So as we're doing our investigation with this guy,

00:05:40.909 --> 00:05:45.099
we ask a bunch of questions and we talk to different
people, and we watch, and we observe over several

00:05:45.099 --> 00:05:49.530
days and see several examples of flipping the
tables. It's not like a one-time deal.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.440
Pretty much the same response every time.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.240
Now, because I'm anchored to this, I can't run around
the room, but

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.369
what would happen typically:

00:05:56.369 --> 00:05:59.110
scream-- stand up, scream "The Rosenberg findout,"

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.060
flip the table,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.199
run to the door,

00:06:02.199 --> 00:06:03.669
but not go through. 

00:06:03.669 --> 00:06:05.549
Stand at the threshold of an open door

00:06:05.549 --> 00:06:06.610
and watch

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:10.739
the chaos going on around him. So you can
imagine, you got kids jumping around, you got teachers

00:06:10.739 --> 00:06:13.460
and teacher aides that are getting crazy.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.520
He's kind of sitting there taking it all in.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.520
In a couple seconds, one of the adults in the
room comes over him and says, "Alright, Joey--

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.259
go sit down over there by the books and
the bean bag chair and just, like, calm down."

00:06:25.259 --> 00:06:27.389
That's the scenario. So we see this

00:06:27.389 --> 00:06:29.659
very consistent pattern of,

00:06:29.659 --> 00:06:34.389
he's at the table, jumps up, screams, flips, runs,
stops-- somebody sends him to the 

00:06:34.389 --> 00:06:37.129
book area to sit in the bean bag chair. It happens six or eight times. 

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:38.859
So we're looking at this and saying, 

00:06:38.859 --> 00:06:41.129
"Wonder what that's all about."

00:06:41.129 --> 00:06:44.309
What are you thinking so far?

00:06:44.309 --> 00:06:47.150
What do you thinks far?

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:52.259
What's going on here?É 

00:06:52.259 --> 00:06:55.260
He wants some kind of a reaction. Good, terrific.
Now,

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:57.189
we talk about reaction--

00:06:57.189 --> 00:06:59.900
from whom?

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.739
Anybody in particular that

00:07:01.739 --> 00:07:04.219
comes into mind for you?

00:07:04.219 --> 00:07:06.300
Maybe the teachers? Sure, it could be the kids.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.710
Could just be

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.990
a reaction, right?

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:12.150
We don't know yet, but you're on the right
track, because there was a behavior and then there 

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.330
is an effect.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.330
We have something that's started and some

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.080
way of having an effect.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.869
We look a little further and we start asking ourselves
questions.

00:07:20.869 --> 00:07:22.759
"What's he doing at the table?"

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:24.559
This is not happening all day long.

00:07:24.559 --> 00:07:26.800
He's not flipping the table every time he sits

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:28.329
down at the table to do work.

00:07:28.329 --> 00:07:29.250
So we watch him. 

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:30.039
He reads.

00:07:30.039 --> 00:07:33.090
He answers questions. He does math worksheets. 

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:37.470
And we start to watch the work at the table
and we notice there's one particular time that this is

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.669
more likely to occur. We're going to call that
a high probability sequence, ok?

00:07:41.669 --> 00:07:45.639
The time when it's most likely to occur is when
he has a worksheet in front of him 

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:48.959
that has an open-ended question, ok? Now, we have

00:07:48.959 --> 00:07:53.659
an example. Closed-based and open-ended questions.
A closed-based question might be like a multiple-choice

00:07:53.659 --> 00:07:54.629
question, alright?

00:07:54.629 --> 00:07:58.960
Where the universe of possible responses-- you know,
A, B, C, and D--

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:00.800
they're defined for you, you don't have to say, "Well,

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:05.039
I'm not sure where to go with this," because
the four choices are right in front of you.

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:10.050
We call those closed-based questions. Open-ended
questions would be like,

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.080
"So, what are you going to do today after you've finished with
this class?"

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:13.990
Dot dot dot. 

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:17.339
Fill in the blank. Write me seven sentences about that, alright?

00:08:17.339 --> 00:08:19.339
It's up to you to decide.

00:08:19.339 --> 00:08:22.360
But it's also incumbent on you to organize. 

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:23.399
Ok? So you have to 

00:08:23.399 --> 00:08:26.399
come up with a gestalt, and then you have to
say, "Ah, well.

00:08:26.399 --> 00:08:27.769
Probably gonna go have supper--

00:08:27.769 --> 00:08:29.419
with who, where.

00:08:29.419 --> 00:08:30.499
Immediately or later.

00:08:30.499 --> 00:08:32.529
I can stop and do some work first."

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:34.940
You have to put that all together.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:36.720
This guy

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.620
had pretty reliable antecedent 

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.839
events that included open-ended

00:08:41.839 --> 00:08:43.889
response situations that

00:08:43.889 --> 00:08:46.520
had a written component. 

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.130
So if he had a teacher sitting next to him saying,

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.290
"So what just happened in this story? Was it
this, this, this, or this?"--

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:53.420
no problema. 

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.130
If he had

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:56.660
open-ended--

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:59.290
"What would you think would happen to the child

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:00.860
if the event didn't occur?

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:02.190
Write it down"--

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:07.780
flip the table, Rosenberg findout, to the door,
to the beanbag chair, look at the books. Ok?

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:13.250
What do you think's going on now?

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:17.530
It may well be to escape from the work, exactly.
It--

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:21.920
good hypothesis, and it's actually the one we're working
with to start. 

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.320
He may be saying,

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:24.890
"I'm looking at this work,

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:26.280
not going to do it,"

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.130
one way to get out of doing the work is to flip

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:34.250
the table and--?

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:35.590
Run to the door, very good...

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:41.130
I'm a behavioral analyst. At the end of this class, I'm going to show you a graph of response latencies. Yours. 

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:48.020
How long it takes you to answer the question when I first ask it. No, I'm just teasing. Ok.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:51.990
He's getting away from this, and he's zipping
to the door, ok? Now,

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:55.430
there's something else going on here, and it's
subtle.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.600
But escape

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.000
from demand

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.540
is certainly a hypothesis that we want to
test out here.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.790
But there's something else that's going on,
because what else is he doing?

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:08.680
He's hitting the door and then what is an
adult saying to him?

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.470
"Go to the beanbag chair and--"?

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:15.060
It could be a reinforcer. Let's
make a distinction between rewards and reinforcers, ok?

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:19.130
This may be a minor one but in my
book it's going to be a fairly major one.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:22.980
"It could be a reinforcer." Have you guys had
any background on the difference between rewards

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:24.350
and reinforcers?

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.800
So you know that reinforcers do what?

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:28.790
Increase the...?

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:30.670
Probability of...?

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:33.770
The behavior occurring in the...?

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:37.840
Future. Ok, that was an intraverbal--that's
very close to a closed-based question, ok?

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:40.260
And you got it. Very good. 

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.519
So what we have with this is somebody

00:10:42.519 --> 00:10:45.000
who says, "Hey, wow. I get out of this--

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.200
'cause I don't wanna do it anyway--

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:48.860
and I get to go sit in a beanbag chair,

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.580
and look at a book."

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.420
Now I want to make a distinction for me.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:55.280
If both of them are possibly reinforcers,

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:00.570
what type of reinforcers would books and beanbag
chairs be?...

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:01.230
Positive. Good.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:06.770
I want to dig a little deeper than
that.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:08.690
You get the same

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:09.850
feedback.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:12.500
You get the same neurological feedback from
reading a book

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:15.670
as sitting in the beanbag chair.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:22.670
Has anybody here not sat in a bean bag chair in their life? Ok, good.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:27.280
What's it like? What's it feel like to sit in a beanbag chair? Say?...

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:28.900
It's relaxing.

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:34.220
What's it feel like, not what's it think like. What does it feel like?

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.490
Comfortable, ok.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.630
Does it kind of mold to your body? 

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.280
Ok? It kind of smooches in?

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:43.630
Can you think of a difference between sitting 
in a beanbag chair and being smooched into that

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:45.020
and having somebody

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:48.960
give a really tight squeeze and a hug?

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.900
You know what kind of sensory information you're getting
from that?

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.730
It's called proprioception.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.850
Ok? Basically joint compression or deep
pressure.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:01.060
The information that goes into your joints
goes into your brain and says, I know where I am physically

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:03.410
in this environment. I'm sort of wrapped, okay?

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:06.890
It's like when we have little tiny babies
who are very disregulated after they're

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:07.410
born?

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:12.950
And even when they're not disregulated? What
do we do with those blankets?

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:17.170
We swaddle them! Exactly. We wrap them up tight.
Do you know why we do that?

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:21.940
'Cause babies have a startle reflex and when they go like this it scares the daylights out of 'em.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:26.680
So you swaddle them, they don't get scared-- they're much calmer, ok?

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:30.620
All these people walking around with babies in papooses
and kangaroo babies and everything else like

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:31.119
this,

00:12:31.119 --> 00:12:32.690
there's meaning to that. Alright.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.430
So what we've got is a guy who's getting a reinforcer,

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:36.950
at two levels: read the book,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:40.150
sit in this really cool chair. Pretty big payoff.

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:42.990
But for what?

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:45.580
Good behavior, bad behavior?

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:46.930
Problem behavior, you betcha.

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:49.140
Now, you're the kid.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:52.840
You're seeing the teacher walk over with the handouts.
She's gonna put them on the table. You take a quick look; you

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:57.690
see it's open-ended. What are you thinking?

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:00.580
You're thinking, I know how to get out of here.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:02.630
And I know how to get what I want,

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:05.470
'cause they consistently give me the bean bag chair
and the books.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:12.470
I like the bean bag chair and the books. There's one very
clear way to say that to my teachers. It is:

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:17.690
Rosenberg findout, flip the table, and they'll
give me my books.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:21.620
So if I'm a person with autism and I'm
saying, "I'd like to look at the books now,"

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:22.470
one way

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:24.880
that I can communicate that

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:26.000
clearly-- perhaps not

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:30.440
adaptively, but clearly-- is do the behavior
that's problematic.

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.120
Everybody understands it.

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:35.110
Therein lies the problem. Ok?

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:39.010
That's the problem we confront. So when we start
talking about what people with autism might do,

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:42.020
what people with developmental disabilities might
do,

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.270
what we're really looking at

00:13:43.270 --> 00:13:47.360
is the purpose behind their behavior. We call
that purpose function. Ok?

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:49.620
And you're going to hear me talk about function,

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.830
functional analysis, functional assessment
pretty much all afternoon.

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:55.249
When we talk about folks 

00:13:55.249 --> 00:13:57.110
with autism spectrum disorders

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:58.340
and behavioral issues,

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:02.240
we have to remember first and foremost: if
you have an autism spectrum disorder, it is

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:09.240
a social learning disability. Yes, ma'am?

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:32.830
Punishing him would not take away the thing
that was troubling him initially, that's right.

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:52.670
How many of you guys are psychology majors?

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:58.650
Who's not? What are you guys in? Spanish? Ok.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.710
Biology, ok.

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.460
Gotcha, ok.

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.490
Biochem, ok.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.720
Everybody in here wants to help somebody, right?

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:08.940
You wanna help them talk--

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.140
speak better Spanish, you wanna

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:14.710
make them better biochemists, or whatever you guys do with
your lives.

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:19.710
You make all of us healthy, which is a good
thing-- in my case, it's a very good idea.

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.410
Here's the thing:

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:25.050
you all want to help people, so you have a
natural tendency to jump to an intervention

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:26.790
before you-- before it's time.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.190
Which is exactly what you did.

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.689
And I'm not faulting you for it,

00:15:29.689 --> 00:15:33.670
because all of us are going to do that-- we're
gonna say, ok, what do we do?

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:35.450
You know what the answer is at this point?

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:36.730
You don't.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:40.270
You understand it better so you don't make mistakes.

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:43.510
Now, you asked a really good question when you
said,

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:47.610
"Giving this guy some sort of punishment procedure is
not likely to address

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:49.830
the trigger, the antecedent, right?

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:51.990
Of the behavior itself.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:55.010
So we could punish him until the cows come home, but
every time he sees that paper,

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.000
he's going to have to think,

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.810
"Maybe I'll take the punishment.

00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:02.390
'Cause I've really had it with this paper stuff."

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:05.290
What if I told you that there are two
other important things we have to do: change the 

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:06.640
antecedent,

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:08.630
and change the adult behavior.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.760
And teach him a new behavior.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:13.970
Now what is flipping the table, screaming "the
Rosenberg findout"

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:17.070
saying?

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:22.480
What's the communicative intent?É Louder?

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:25.960
Before that. Before--

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.420
who?

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:32.209
"I don't want to do this work." You said it before.
"I don't want to do this paper."

00:16:32.209 --> 00:16:33.390
Maybe it's also saying,

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:36.040
"I wanna go sit in the beanbag chair and read the book."

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:38.110
Let's just say for argument's sake,

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:39.160
initially,

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:40.129
it's saying

00:16:40.129 --> 00:16:43.600
"I don't want to do this work" or "I don't know
how to do this work."

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:47.480
Fair enough? Are we good?

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:49.930
Tell me an alternative

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:50.550
response

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:52.810
that you, as that student,

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:54.690
could emit, could do,

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:55.380
to say,

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:58.430
"I don't want to do it" or "I need help."

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:01.030
What could you do that would be socially acceptable?

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:02.610
That's exactly right.

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:04.429
Ok. Did you

00:17:04.429 --> 00:17:11.429
have some kind of a class when were a little kid
to teach you hand-raising?É Did you really?

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.410
É Where'd you learn it?

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:18.120
Did you pick it up in kindergarten?

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:21.959
You probably did, ok?

00:17:21.959 --> 00:17:25.279
And why are you using it now?

00:17:25.279 --> 00:17:30.590
Most of you, when you had a question, raised
your hand. Why?

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:34.620
Yeah, you're not that nice. Why do you use it?

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:36.380
Because it works.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.150
It's functional.

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.549
It's what we call a pivotal response.

00:17:40.549 --> 00:17:44.340
I know what hand-raising means. You raise your hand,
I'm gonna say,

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:47.210
"You have something you wanna ask, 'cause you
need help,

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:49.730
or you don't understand,

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.770
or you gotta use the restroom. Something. 

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.100
But it's an attention cue, it's an attention signal, and

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:55.909
I know what it means.

00:17:55.909 --> 00:17:59.180
I didn't take a class in hand-raising any more
than you did.

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:02.490
But I did learn it by observation,

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:03.050
modeling,

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:04.770
and reinforcement, ok?

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:06.380
I didn't learn it by punishment.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:09.980
I learned it because when I raised my hand, I got somebody's attention

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:13.530
faster than when I flipped a table, screamed "the
Rosenberg findout,"

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:17.179
When we start talking communicative intent, we
have to go-- to get to your question-- we have

00:18:17.179 --> 00:18:21.919
to go back to, what is he trying to say to us? That's
why I said to you, behavior is communication.

00:18:21.919 --> 00:18:23.020
He is saying,

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:24.770
"Out of here, need help,

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:25.970
can't do it."

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:28.679
If he raised his hand without flipping the table,

00:18:28.679 --> 00:18:34.050
what would be the most likely thing that you
as a teacher would do?

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:39.690
Would you ignore him?

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:41.040
You're not sure. 

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:45.970
Don't teach Spanish if you would ignore him. Not good. What's that?... 

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:47.880
É You would walk over. You'd go,

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:52.050
allow him to solicit your attention, your 
assistance, and you would do something about it.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:53.590
Now if you did something,

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:55.870
and it made work easier for him,

00:18:55.870 --> 00:18:57.010
what's his payoff? 

00:18:57.010 --> 00:19:00.400
i.e. what's his reinforcer?

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:04.660
If you help them with the work, is it easier
or harder than if he does it himself?

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:08.130
It's easier, ok. If it's easier, that means
there's less effort on his part,

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:09.810
with greater success.

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:11.130
You will do the things

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.140
that are easier for you to do

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:17.040
and also have them maintain high correct response rates
of success, ok?

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.510
You'll do that, alright?

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:23.060
Organisms do that. They go for what's easy and
what works best.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:26.620
That's all we have to work with. If you take
that example, pretty much everything we talk

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:30.220
about the rest of the afternoon is going to start to hang
together.

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:32.420
We good with your question? Ok?

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:34.070
This is a social learning disability.

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:36.780
A lot of behavior in autism--

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:40.770
and because I know you heard a few lectures before
I got here, you know that the range of capacity

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:41.680
in autism

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:42.950
is tremendous.

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:46.010
Some of you are working at Chapel Haven and so you've
got

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:49.430
some individuals who are-- to say that they're
high-functioning is an understatement. Some of these

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:53.860
folks, you know, wrote the book on IQ. And they
can do things in their heads that we can't

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:55.880
conceive of. On the other hand,

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:59.660
they may do that with absolutely no understanding
that there's a social engagement that goes

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:02.280
on with this, that you have to

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:04.540
participate in some meaningful way to share

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:07.400
what you know and are doing with someone so
they

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.120
wanna do it back with you.

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:14.340
The social learning disability and the misunderstanding
of social context is one of the biggest problems

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:17.300
that we deal with in autism that affects the
behavioral issues.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:19.330
The second thing is--
0:20:19.330,0:20:21.200--
and another thing I'm sure you've heard is

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:23.970
that folks with autism don't necessarily

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:25.629
know that you're thinking

00:20:25.629 --> 00:20:27.330
the way you are;

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:30.060
they may be thinking you think like they think.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.610
Now I know that was a rather convoluted thing,
but I want you to think of

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:37.500
having to take someone's perspective and what
that requires you to do, ok?

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:41.520
It requires you to think about how someone
else is experiencing something

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:42.940
as opposed to your

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:44.279
immediate experience.

00:20:44.279 --> 00:20:46.440
And the importance of that

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:50.960
as a core issue in the social disability
of the autism spectrum really can't

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:52.300
be understated.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:54.159
So if we have someone who

00:20:54.159 --> 00:20:59.460
says, "Well, what's wrong with flipping the table and screaming
'the Rosenberg findout'?"

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:01.409
Well, it's not exactly what you see in school. 

00:21:01.409 --> 00:21:05.080
And then if I said to you, "What's wrong with
'the Rosenberg findout' as a phrase?"

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:08.640
What would you say? 

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:13.220
Why is that a bad communicative strategy?

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:16.160
Doesn't communicate anything about what you want
or need, true enough. It doesn't--

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.130
well, if I had autism,

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:20.570
and that's my phrase.

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:22.640
Works for me, right? 

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:23.800
My blindness is--

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:27.620
the person with autism saying, "It works for me,
it must work for you.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.500
You read my mind.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.200
You know my mind. My blindness is: 

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:36.160
I don't know that you don't, ok?

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:38.270
The phrase "the Rosenberg findout"

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:40.240
is very specific type

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:43.850
of social communication problem; it's called a neologism. 

00:21:43.850 --> 00:21:45.370
It's a word made up

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:49.540
by someone to mean something.

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:53.350
So we have lots of neologisms in autism;
they go all over the place, all over the map,

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:56.690
but the bottom line is the person with autism 
is the thinking, "You know what I'm trying to

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:57.820
say here"

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:00.640
and you as the teacher-- you as the student sitting next to him are saying,

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:02.910
"I don't know what's going on.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:03.740
This guy's wild."

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:05.929
Ok? "This is dangerous, to be around here."

00:22:05.929 --> 00:22:09.649
The third thing is that executive functions are
generally

00:22:09.649 --> 00:22:10.290
compromised

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:11.529
in the world of autism.

00:22:11.529 --> 00:22:14.850
So if you have somebody who has difficulty

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:19.670
looking at a situation and deciding what's
the most relevant piece of information here.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.780
You have somebody who

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:23.570
doesn't necessarily take

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:25.720
experiences from the past that they've had

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:29.810
and apply them to the situation in a somewhat
flexible or modified. way. 

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:31.710
If somebody who doesn't

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:35.789
scan the environment for somebody who's being
successful and say, "I would just hang around

00:22:35.789 --> 00:22:37.250
that successful person

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:39.660
and I'll do ok."

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:42.789
That's the problem of executive functions,
and they're rampant

00:22:42.789 --> 00:22:43.920
in autism.

00:22:43.920 --> 00:22:45.190
The fourth thing is that

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:49.830
we have this tendency towards rigidity, and
if you've done anything with Benhaven-- are

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:54.840
some of you guys going to Benhaven in Wallingford, to
the school?

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:58.250
With Karen Lee? Ok.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:01.230
And some of your-- some of you going over to

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.300
the residence?

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:04.510
You've seen some

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:08.090
folks with autism who are a little rigid?

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:11.330
You know what behavioral rigidity-- certainly you've seen that
with some of the individuals at

00:23:11.330 --> 00:23:13.360
Chapel Haven who are very capable,

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:16.540
you know? Things have to be a certain way?

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.280
That rigidity--

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:20.049
that's not peskiness.

00:23:20.049 --> 00:23:23.490
That's like air. That's a necessity to organize
oneself

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.200
to respond, ok?

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.929
That's something that's very important to
the person. Now

00:23:27.929 --> 00:23:30.840
that might lead you to think, well, fine if
that's what they need, let's give it to them.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:34.850
But rigidity isn't very adaptive in the environment because
if you're rigid

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:36.049
in your

00:23:36.049 --> 00:23:38.500
access to the environment, you can only access

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:41.180
highly predictable environments,

00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:44.400
which means you can only go to the places
that you really know and understand.

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:48.200
You don't get out of your orbit, your
environment,

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:52.540
and if you think about what a lot of people
with autism do, is they fail in novel environments.

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:57.310
They could look really good in familiar environments,
but they fail in novel environments, and that

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.870
particular problem is called a lack of fluency.

00:23:59.870 --> 00:24:03.500
You can't use your skill in a variety of novel
settings,

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:06.720
and it doesn't serve folks with autism very well.

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:10.600
When we think about behavioral interventions, we
absolutely

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:11.280
positively

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:14.350
wrap it around learning and social environments

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:16.540
for assessment and for intervention--

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:19.240
treatment, planning, and development.

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:23.140
The behavior has lots of purposes. The stuff
on the left,

00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:25.710
from aggression all the way down to motor tasks--

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:27.340
that's what you're going to call us about.

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:29.450
You're going to say, "I've got a kid and he bites."

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:32.580
Or "I've got a kid and he flaps his fingers and he does
this," or

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:36.640
"He kind of"-- this is a great room for this,
if you autism, you'd have a field day

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:38.169
in here, perhaps, because

00:24:38.169 --> 00:24:41.170
we've got verticals, we've got fluorescent lights,

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:44.480
I can do all kinds of things and make patterns
with my hands,

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:47.400
I can make this room very visually stimulating

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.370
without even talking to you guys.

00:24:49.370 --> 00:24:51.050
And I could sit here and do this

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:53.410
and really get off on it and enjoy it,

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:54.990
and not be thinking,

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:57.730
does this group of students think I'm nuts?

00:24:57.730 --> 00:25:00.529
Do they think that this is just not what the
professor does?

00:25:00.529 --> 00:25:02.420
And not only not think it,

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:04.720
not care to know.

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:06.090
'Cause I'm more involved

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:08.280
in getting this type of visual

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:10.530
sensory feedback that I'm looking for, ok?

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.540
That's part of the problem we see, but

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:16.850
we often get these requests, or these
calls to consult or solve

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:21.480
the problem, when people identify the behavior
in question. So they say, we've got aggression, or we

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:24.220
have pica. You know what pica is?

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:25.480
Ingesting inedibles?

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:27.850
So people who eat cigarette butts?

00:25:27.850 --> 00:25:30.000
Not chew on; swallow.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.340
Ok? Or coins. 

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:33.029
Rocks, stones?

00:25:33.029 --> 00:25:34.659
You know, it's fairly dangerous behavior.

00:25:34.659 --> 00:25:37.120
We're not talking about people who chew their shirt.

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:39.770
We're talking about ingestion.

00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:42.980
Self-injury, noncompliance, you know you
name it.

00:25:42.980 --> 00:25:48.030
Those behaviors are problematic, they get
in the way, they're stigmatizing, they're dangerous, they're--

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:50.270
minimally, they're annoying.

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:52.770
And what we've done some research on looking
at--

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:55.940
so, when we have behaviors, where do they go? We
find that

00:25:55.940 --> 00:26:00.529
for the majority of individuals with autism
spectrum disorders, escape from demands

00:26:00.529 --> 00:26:02.559
is the most likely function,

00:26:02.559 --> 00:26:06.330
purpose behind, reason for the behavior.

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:07.920
So we find that people will do

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:11.059
behaviors to get out of other things that you want them to do.

00:26:11.059 --> 00:26:12.669
Fair enough

00:26:12.669 --> 00:26:13.950
We also find that

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:17.650
when we look across a whole-- and this is
about--

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:21.820
we're talking about a couple hundred
individuals in this particular sample

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:25.170
of a study done a number of years ago--

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:26.690
if you just take

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:28.160
access to tangibles,

00:26:28.160 --> 00:26:29.029
ok, which is:

00:26:29.029 --> 00:26:33.250
I want a certain thing and if you don't give it
to me, I'm going to have a fit.

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:37.520
You've got almost fifty percent--forty four
and a half percent of the individuals

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:39.129
in this particular surveying--

00:26:39.129 --> 00:26:42.330
their behavior was maintained by, triggered
by,

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:43.809
wanting access or wanting escape.

00:26:43.809 --> 00:26:47.520
"I want what I want, and I don't want to do what
you're asking me to do."

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:50.770
That is a very common finding in the world
of autism.

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:51.860
We do have

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:53.630
people, however, who

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:56.870
engage in behavior problems to access social
attention.

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:58.820
They do it for purposes of

00:26:58.820 --> 00:26:59.860
obtaining

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:02.410
sensory feedback, or what we call stereotypies. 

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:05.750
And then there's a lot of behavior that's multiply 
functioned. We think about the

00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:07.250
Rosenberg findout--

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:10.030
that behavior was actually multiply functioned, ok?

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:12.020
You said it's escape from the task, 

00:27:12.020 --> 00:27:15.470
and young lady in the back said maybe he wanted
the access

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:19.540
to the attention or the reading of the
book or sitting in the beanbag chair. 

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:20.710
Perfectly reasonable.

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:23.430
This is negative reinforcement.

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.100
You're talking about positive reinforcement.

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.130
And by the way, why is

00:27:28.130 --> 00:27:29.850
getting out of doing the dirty work

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:31.980
with the open-ended question negative reinforcement?

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:35.180
And not punishment?

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:36.350
Have you guys

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:39.240
tackled that yet?

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:40.740
Who drives a car?

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:41.680
Whether here

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:44.600
at university or somewhere else?

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:46.850
All of you have seatbelts?

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:50.420
What happens if you don't put the seatbelt on?

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:56.650
What happens if you turn the ignition on, you don't put the seatbelt on?

00:27:56.650 --> 00:27:59.540
The car dings at you. You have some sort of an audible signal?

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:01.160
Most of you.

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:03.140
Ok, that's good, you're driving cars that are

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:05.670
post-1970, that's a good thing. 

00:28:05.670 --> 00:28:09.850
So you have a noise that happens. You like
the noise?

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:12.020
Do any of you like the noise?

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:18.060
What do you need to do to make the noise go away? Sorry?É You've gotta

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:21.360
put on your seatbelt and click it, right?

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:26.850
What is the aversive stimulus? The noise.

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:30.290
What do you do to terminate the aversive stimulus?

00:28:30.290 --> 00:28:31.460
You put the seatbelt on.

00:28:31.460 --> 00:28:34.480
Is wearing your seat belt considered a positive
or a

00:28:34.480 --> 00:28:38.360
negative behavior? Is it appropriate or not appropriate?

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:41.670
Ok, so, negative reinforcement

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:44.640
is terminating an aversive stimulus, alright?

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:45.509
In order to 

00:28:45.509 --> 00:28:48.620
get reinforcement. What's your reinforcement when you put
the

00:28:48.620 --> 00:28:50.940
seat belt on?

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:55.490
It stops, exactly. The noise goes away. So
you're saying, "Thank god, all I have to do is click my seatbelt

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:57.800
to make this aversive contingency go away."

00:28:57.800 --> 00:28:59.440
So what we've got is the 

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:03.320
removal of an aversive contingency

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:07.330
that you have control over, and you do something
good to do that, and that's a good

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:11.080
pro-social behavior, but it operates in the context
of negative reinforcement, which is exactly what's

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:12.179
going on here.

00:29:12.179 --> 00:29:15.340
Now the tricky little thing is, as soon as you do that, this kid

00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:17.290
flips the table

00:29:17.290 --> 00:29:21.000
and he runs over there and they tell him to sit down
in the chair--

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:24.330
we have two things going on. He's reinforced because
he doesn't have to do the work,

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.200
you tell him to go sit down in the chair, now he's quiet,

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:28.110
everything's calm...

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:30.270
You, the adult, are reinforced.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:33.010
We have a very dangerous trap going on here

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:37.600
that people fall into all the time. And it even has a term: it's
called a negative reinforcement trap.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:39.190
More on that a little bit later.

00:29:39.190 --> 00:29:42.620
But at the end of the day, if we don't analyze
these individual

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:44.490
what we call contingencies,

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:45.670
you might say,

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.070
"Don't flip the table, I'm going to put you in time-out,"

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:53.919
which will not change this behavior over the
long term. It will lead to symptom substitution.

00:29:53.919 --> 00:29:58.530
All kinds of problems, all kinds of consequences.
You can have educational access that you lose,

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:03.310
you can have social access that you lose.
You can get into trouble with the law. You

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:03.880
can

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:10.880
break things and have people make you pay for them. Please?...

00:30:29.870 --> 00:30:36.870
When you say the word "they," you mean folks with
autism here?É Got it.

00:30:49.350 --> 00:30:55.730
It makes perfect sense. Is that the intent--
is that what you took from what I said?

00:30:55.730 --> 00:30:56.720
Ok. You got it, ok?

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:00.600
everybody in this room response a negative
reinforcement

00:31:00.600 --> 00:31:03.920
Everybody in this room responds to negative reinforcement contingencies, ok? And everyone in this room seeks positive reinforcement contingencies.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:07.850
And some of you don't manage them well, and sometimes you're more
negative and people

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:11.850
leave you alone, and if you like being left alone
that's a really good strategy, except that you won't have

00:31:11.850 --> 00:31:13.200
many friends after a while.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:14.119
On the other hand,

00:31:14.119 --> 00:31:16.819
if you really want to be around people, and
you're crabby all the time,

00:31:16.819 --> 00:31:18.410
you don't get what you want.

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:22.179
The key difference in here-- the one distinction that
I want to make is-- you had something in

00:31:22.179 --> 00:31:26.850
which you may or may not have intended to
say in the same way, which, is of lower intelligence.

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:29.520
Don't make an assumption about intelligence

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:31.310
when you talk about autism.

00:31:31.310 --> 00:31:34.420
Because-- there's a term that I'm going to use with you,
and you may have heard this before too:

00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:36.530
multiple intelligences.

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:38.300
I want you to think of people with autism

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:42.790
from the context of multiple intelligences
rather than a global intelligence

00:31:42.790 --> 00:31:44.960
factor or something like gene,

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:47.390
okay-- that we would say, "Well, there is an IQ."

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:50.220
The world of autism is far too variable for that.

00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:53.380
It's much more common to have somebody who's got

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:58.360
absolutely breathtaking visual perceptual skills
and can see something,

00:31:58.360 --> 00:31:59.420
close their eyes,

00:31:59.420 --> 00:32:02.480
and reproduce the schematic of that

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:06.060
like this.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.040
But have horrifically bad verbal skills and can't have
a conversation.

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:11.700
The literature is full of individuals
like this,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:15.310
so what I want us to be thinking about is
people with

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:19.559
intelligences or domains of intelligence
and domains of deficit, ok?

00:32:19.559 --> 00:32:20.460
Rather than a global.

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:21.440
And don't know 

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:23.970
if you're at Chapel Haven or Benhaven,

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:26.860
but if you're at Chapel Haven you're running across
some folks

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:29.369
who are, you know, working on six cylinders

00:32:29.369 --> 00:32:30.710
in some areas.

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:34.990
And even if you're at Benhaven with some
individuals who are very significantly compromised,

00:32:34.990 --> 00:32:36.710
you also know that there are

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:38.719
some fellows there, and women there,

00:32:38.719 --> 00:32:40.410
that can do things you say,

00:32:40.410 --> 00:32:42.030
"How can that be?"

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:43.210
The person

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:46.730
can't handle any situation that demands flexibility;
they have no language.

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:48.230
A lot of behavioral problems.

00:32:48.230 --> 00:32:50.309
Yet they can still--

00:32:50.309 --> 00:32:52.950
whatever they can do that's exceptionally
good.

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:55.720
Really that's the paradox of autism.

00:32:55.720 --> 00:33:00.500
It's what makes it so uniquely different
than intellectual disability in its most

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:01.269
straightforward form,

00:33:01.269 --> 00:33:03.160
is the variability. And

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:05.380
if you're somebody like me-- and you know,

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:09.460
this is thirty six years that I've been
working with kids in the autism spectrum,

00:33:09.460 --> 00:33:11.740
adults in the autism spectrum,

00:33:11.740 --> 00:33:15.590
that's the thing that makes me get up everyday.
That's the wow factor. And it's like,

00:33:15.590 --> 00:33:16.900
"How'd you do that?

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.900
That's really cool.

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:20.960
Well, to this we move to

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:22.549
some basic assumptions

00:33:22.549 --> 00:33:26.119
that build upon something that you were saying, which
is, if behavior is communication,

00:33:26.119 --> 00:33:30.639
then we also recognize that behavior is a relationship
between the person and the environment.

00:33:30.639 --> 00:33:32.429
Behavior doesn't occur in a vacuum.

00:33:32.429 --> 00:33:35.970
And when I talk about environment, I'm not just
talking about physical environment, I could be talking

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:38.799
about internal environment.

00:33:38.799 --> 00:33:42.920
Anybody here had the flu yet-- hope not?

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:44.150
Did ya? Ouch. Ok.

00:33:44.150 --> 00:33:45.920
In the early part of your flu--

00:33:45.920 --> 00:33:47.210
you guys who have had the flu--

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:48.800
did you go to class

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:51.710
some days when you started to get sick?

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:53.950
Were you at the top of your game?

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:54.719
Probably not, right?

00:33:54.719 --> 00:33:56.049
'Cause you're probably feeling kind of funky

00:33:56.049 --> 00:33:59.809
but you're not even sure why you're not feeling
good, and then all of a sudden you're real sick?

00:33:59.809 --> 00:34:04.990
Ok. Then you finally, hopefully, stop going to
class and got a little better? Maybe not?

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:07.400
You could have an internal environment.

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:09.089
Illness is 

00:34:09.089 --> 00:34:11.540
an environmental setting-- we call them a setting event--

00:34:11.540 --> 00:34:13.589
can affect your performance.

00:34:13.589 --> 00:34:16.339
So if you had been really doing well in a class
and studying

00:34:16.339 --> 00:34:21.219
and getting great grades on tests and quizzes and you
go sick and take a test and bomb--

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:23.009
have you become dumb?

00:34:23.009 --> 00:34:23.729
Or

00:34:23.729 --> 00:34:27.489
is it your illness that's interfering with
your attention, focus, and the short term working 

00:34:27.489 --> 00:34:28.329
memory?

00:34:28.329 --> 00:34:30.069
Probably the latter.

00:34:30.069 --> 00:34:32.229
We call the illness a setting event,

00:34:32.229 --> 00:34:33.669
or an establishing operation.

00:34:33.669 --> 00:34:35.709
More on that later.

00:34:35.709 --> 00:34:39.049
But it is an important variable, and if you don't
think of those variables,

00:34:39.049 --> 00:34:40.299
you'll be saying,

00:34:40.299 --> 00:34:43.200
"Everything is outside,

00:34:43.200 --> 00:34:47.039
it's peripheral to the person with autism," when we have
some internal issues.

00:34:47.039 --> 00:34:48.349
We have people with

00:34:48.349 --> 00:34:51.640
neurocognitive deficits-- they have right
brain/left brain

00:34:51.640 --> 00:34:52.989
discrepancies. We have people with

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:54.979
poor right hemisphere

00:34:54.979 --> 00:34:56.989
abilities and great left hemisphere abilities

00:34:56.989 --> 00:34:58.519
and vice versa.

00:34:58.519 --> 00:34:59.919
If you know that,

00:34:59.919 --> 00:35:03.559
you have a way of thinking about how this
person with autism thinks.

00:35:03.559 --> 00:35:05.140
If you don't know that,

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:07.229
you're throwing mud at the wall and seeing what sticks.

00:35:07.229 --> 00:35:08.979
You're hoping you can figure it out.

00:35:08.979 --> 00:35:12.640
So as we gather this kind of information, the more we
get the better we are.

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:13.829
If you're going to intervene,

00:35:13.829 --> 00:35:17.259
you address the variables that are functional
to the situation,

00:35:17.259 --> 00:35:19.880
which is why--

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:23.449
in the situation you describe, we got a
guy who's trying to escape from a demand, and

00:35:23.449 --> 00:35:28.069
we say, "Fine, you wanna escape, I'm giving you a time-out."

00:35:28.069 --> 00:35:29.510
Now, I want to put yourself in

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:32.869
the place of the person with autism in that situation. Right?

00:35:32.869 --> 00:35:35.079
You don't wanna do this.

00:35:35.079 --> 00:35:37.089
You get upset, you say, "I don't wanna do this." You misbehave.

00:35:37.089 --> 00:35:44.089
I say, "Fine, time-out for you." What have I just done? Really. Come on.

00:35:47.769 --> 00:35:50.359
Were you happy to get the time-out?

00:35:50.359 --> 00:35:53.199
Or were you not so happy?

00:35:53.199 --> 00:35:54.049
Yeah-- why?

00:35:54.049 --> 00:35:56.029
You're happy; why?

00:35:56.029 --> 00:35:59.900
'Cause you don't wanna do it anyway! So what did i do
functionally for you

00:35:59.900 --> 00:36:04.329
by giving you time-out?

00:36:04.329 --> 00:36:06.269
I gave you an escape.

00:36:06.269 --> 00:36:09.889
I run the risk that I just gave you a 
functional reinforcer, don't I? 

00:36:09.889 --> 00:36:11.529
Ok? Because it

00:36:11.529 --> 00:36:13.449
increases the likelihood that you're going
to

00:36:13.449 --> 00:36:17.309
try to get out of doing the work in the same way, 'cause you're
going to get the time-out.

00:36:17.309 --> 00:36:18.870
So we have someone who

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:23.559
does a misbehavior, we say, "Fine, time-out for you,"
and the person's going, "Yes!"

00:36:23.559 --> 00:36:24.599
Or they understand me.

00:36:24.599 --> 00:36:27.019
Communication works for this.

00:36:27.019 --> 00:36:29.380
Those are the pitfalls that we're

00:36:29.380 --> 00:36:31.670
we're really trying to stay on top of, because

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:34.539
it happens regularly.

00:36:34.539 --> 00:36:39.109
Ok, let's hope for the best.

00:36:39.109 --> 00:36:41.749
Émake this work.

00:36:41.749 --> 00:36:48.749
Now this is really the basics of what we're
talking about and I want you to watch.

00:38:06.989 --> 00:38:13.989
What's going on? Hm?...

00:38:25.339 --> 00:38:29.759
What's the problem-- what's the target behavior?

00:38:29.759 --> 00:38:35.929
What's the problem behavior here?

00:38:35.929 --> 00:38:41.339
The child is doing the problem behavior-- what
is it?...

00:38:41.339 --> 00:38:44.029
Yeah, it's having a tantrum.

00:38:44.029 --> 00:38:48.079
Nobody's ever seen this before, right? You've never
seen a tantrum. Ok.

00:38:48.079 --> 00:38:51.819
Come to my house.

00:38:51.819 --> 00:38:54.519
Ok, we have tantrum behavior.

00:38:54.519 --> 00:38:57.499
What is the learning contingency

00:38:57.499 --> 00:39:04.499
that is maintaining that tantrum?É Sorry?É 

00:39:06.149 --> 00:39:12.869
He gets to stay on the camera. You think it's being
on camera. Who else?

00:39:12.869 --> 00:39:14.200
He's getting adult attention,

00:39:14.200 --> 00:39:17.979
in the form of..? For him?

00:39:17.979 --> 00:39:20.649
He perceivesÉ?

00:39:20.649 --> 00:39:22.279
Positive attention or negative attention?

00:39:22.279 --> 00:39:24.969
Probably positive attention. It's positive reinforcement.

00:39:24.969 --> 00:39:26.690
Alright, so, positive reinforcement

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:29.630
through social attention here. Right.

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:33.609
And what this little guy is thinking is, "Hey, whenever
I see these people, when I fall on the floor, this is

00:39:33.609 --> 00:39:35.150
going to work."

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:36.189
Fair enough?

00:39:36.189 --> 00:39:39.779
You watched about seven sequences; I can show you twelve more if you want to see them, because it goes on for

00:39:39.779 --> 00:39:40.829
a little while.

00:39:40.829 --> 00:39:44.380
Now at the end of the class there's going to be
a little jar over here; I want to

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:47.009
put spare change in it, because it's going to be his therapy fund.

00:39:47.009 --> 00:39:49.709
'Cause this kid's gonna need it when he's older; he's
basically--

00:39:49.709 --> 00:39:51.349
for infinity he's on--

00:39:51.349 --> 00:39:54.680
on YouTube here.

00:39:54.680 --> 00:39:59.490
This is a very straightforward example of
problem behavior maintained by social attention.

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:00.380
When we

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:04.139
as clinicians are looking to understand problem
behavior, we say

00:40:04.139 --> 00:40:06.679
"What happens after the problem behavior

00:40:06.679 --> 00:40:09.759
to increase the likelihood of its future occurrence?

00:40:09.759 --> 00:40:11.249
And what is setting the stage for it?"

00:40:11.249 --> 00:40:13.229
Now what if I said this kid is

00:40:13.229 --> 00:40:15.239
super tired,

00:40:15.239 --> 00:40:16.129
versus

00:40:16.129 --> 00:40:17.819
just got up from his nap and he's

00:40:17.819 --> 00:40:20.910
really well rested and well-fed and he's pretty
robust?

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:25.909
What if I told you that in this situation,
the kid is super, super tired?

00:40:25.909 --> 00:40:32.909
What would part of your intervention include?

00:40:33.509 --> 00:40:36.439
Letting the poor little guy have a nap, right?

00:40:36.439 --> 00:40:39.789
All right. But what if I said to you,

00:40:39.789 --> 00:40:42.469
not tired, he's not hungry,

00:40:42.469 --> 00:40:44.809
he has access to all kinds of good stuff.

00:40:44.809 --> 00:40:46.759
Then what would part of 

00:40:46.759 --> 00:40:49.289
your intervention include, or not include? The naps, right?

00:40:49.289 --> 00:40:54.299
If we want to change its behavior, what is one
of the things we have to do?

00:40:54.299 --> 00:40:56.160
Change the relationship

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:57.140
between when he has tantrums

00:40:57.140 --> 00:41:00.029
and when he does or doesn't get attention.

00:41:00.029 --> 00:41:01.709
That is a basic--

00:41:01.709 --> 00:41:04.249
that's a basic intervention strategy here.

00:41:04.249 --> 00:41:06.759
So that's one of the things we're going
to be thinking about.

00:41:06.759 --> 00:41:09.569
When we look at behavioral assessment and treatment, it's

00:41:09.569 --> 00:41:15.559
not a linear process. It's really a circular
process that never ends until you decide

00:41:15.559 --> 00:41:18.239
that you have a replacement behavior

00:41:18.239 --> 00:41:20.449
that's accessible to the child

00:41:20.449 --> 00:41:24.929
or the adult with autism, and it's fluent,
meaning it can be used in a variety of novel

00:41:24.929 --> 00:41:26.190
situations.

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:30.329
When you assess, you get information, you develop
a treatment plan, you go ahead and do the intervention,

00:41:30.329 --> 00:41:35.390
plant a new tree, and then finally you move to an
evaluation phase. And the evaluation gives you

00:41:35.390 --> 00:41:37.609
information that reinforms your assessment.

00:41:37.609 --> 00:41:39.499
When you're reinforcing assessment,

00:41:39.499 --> 00:41:43.360
you're continuously changing what's going on.

00:41:43.360 --> 00:41:45.339
Now you can imagine, in the case of that little guy,

00:41:45.339 --> 00:41:47.410
we change one thing.

00:41:47.410 --> 00:41:51.410
What if I said to the parents, "Alright,
I want you to put this behavior on extinction.

00:41:51.410 --> 00:41:53.669
No attention whatsoever."

00:41:53.669 --> 00:41:56.630
From what you know about basic principles
of extinction,

00:41:56.630 --> 00:42:01.150
what is that behavior likely to do in the
beginning?

00:42:01.150 --> 00:42:07.469
Exactly. Why is it gonna get worse, and what do we call it?

00:42:07.469 --> 00:42:09.329
We call it an extinction burst.

00:42:09.329 --> 00:42:11.419
Why is it going to get worse?

00:42:11.419 --> 00:42:13.599
Think like the kid.

00:42:13.599 --> 00:42:15.799
Don't think like an adult.

00:42:15.799 --> 00:42:20.299
Think like an eighteen month old kid.

00:42:20.299 --> 00:42:22.559
"Hey. They didn't hear my scream.

00:42:22.559 --> 00:42:24.790
These poor people, they must be doing something
else.

00:42:24.790 --> 00:42:26.849
I'm going to ramp it up six or seven notches,

00:42:26.849 --> 00:42:29.339
then they'll really pay attention to me."

00:42:29.339 --> 00:42:31.069
That's kid-think.

00:42:31.069 --> 00:42:32.539
That's the way these things work.

00:42:32.539 --> 00:42:35.009
That's why extinction bursts work, alright?

00:42:35.009 --> 00:42:38.169
What's the worst thing you can do with an extinction burst?

00:42:38.169 --> 00:42:41.150
Behavior's climbing; what's the worst thing you do?

00:42:41.150 --> 00:42:42.989
Give attention as it's climbing.

00:42:42.989 --> 00:42:44.019
You know why?

00:42:44.019 --> 00:42:46.959
You just reinforced persistence. 

00:42:46.959 --> 00:42:49.939
You know why? 'Cause the kid's saying, "Alright, the little
stuff, it doesn't get what I want; the big

00:42:49.939 --> 00:42:53.619
stuff works. The next time, I'm going right to the top."

00:42:53.619 --> 00:42:54.699
Kids are smart.

00:42:54.699 --> 00:42:56.099
Way smarter than we are.

00:42:56.099 --> 00:42:59.840
So that's what's going to happen with it. If you
think about this as you're going through

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:02.239
this rubric for,

00:43:02.239 --> 00:43:04.630
figure it out, develop a treatment,

00:43:04.630 --> 00:43:05.930
evaluate the treatment,

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:09.180
reinform your assessment and treatment plan as
you go,

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:11.169
you'll be getting the right idea.

00:43:11.169 --> 00:43:15.779
When we do behavioral assessment, we do behavioral 
assessment for three reasons: we wanna predict

00:43:15.779 --> 00:43:17.459
what's going on

00:43:17.459 --> 00:43:19.369
as we intervene--

00:43:19.369 --> 00:43:23.029
we want to be able to say, "Alright, I know what's going
on, I have a pretty good plan

00:43:23.029 --> 00:43:24.679
but I wanna inform

00:43:24.679 --> 00:43:26.909
my ongoing intervention. That's that

00:43:26.909 --> 00:43:28.830
circular loop of,

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:32.899
new information adds to my thinking about my treatment
plan and its effect.

00:43:32.899 --> 00:43:36.489
And we wanna provide this information so we can
make readjustments.

00:43:36.489 --> 00:43:39.259
We them want to be able to do a summative evaluation

00:43:39.259 --> 00:43:41.319
in our behavioral assessment

00:43:41.319 --> 00:43:42.379
formula which says,

00:43:42.379 --> 00:43:44.920
I understand what happened.

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:46.149
Now there's good reasons

00:43:46.149 --> 00:43:50.169
for having a summative assessment, even
when the problem is now gone.

00:43:50.169 --> 00:43:51.230
Particularly in autism.

00:43:51.230 --> 00:43:53.069
Because we can have behaviors

00:43:53.069 --> 00:43:54.569
that change

00:43:54.569 --> 00:43:55.259
form

00:43:55.259 --> 00:43:57.549
but retain function.

00:43:57.549 --> 00:44:01.660
Now let me explain what that means.

00:44:01.660 --> 00:44:05.150
Let's say instead of flipping the tables, I engage
in self-injury.

00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:07.449
Ok? And I slap myself in the face.

00:44:07.449 --> 00:44:10.749
Ok? And I slap myself in the face, and I slap myself in the face.

00:44:10.749 --> 00:44:12.729
You get rid of face-slapping.

00:44:12.729 --> 00:44:16.039
But now what I'm doing is, I'm biting my wrist.

00:44:16.039 --> 00:44:19.539
And I'm biting my wrist. And then you get rid
of that one, and then I'm getting into property

00:44:19.539 --> 00:44:21.639
destruction.

00:44:21.639 --> 00:44:26.349
That kind of a sequence of substituting an
alternative form

00:44:26.349 --> 00:44:28.589
happens when we don't address the function.

00:44:28.589 --> 00:44:32.039
So if I don't get at the very basic fact that
this guy

00:44:32.039 --> 00:44:33.540
in the Rosenberg findout

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:35.269
cannot do open-ended

00:44:35.269 --> 00:44:36.779
grapho-motor tasks,

00:44:36.779 --> 00:44:38.419
if I don't get at that problem,

00:44:38.419 --> 00:44:40.560
I will likely have new behaviors

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:44.019
that substitute for flipping the table and
screaming "the Rosenberg findout."

00:44:44.019 --> 00:44:47.430
I'll something else. However,

00:44:47.430 --> 00:44:49.840
if I intervene at the level

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:54.749
of the complexity or the processing
demand of the problem itself,

00:44:54.749 --> 00:44:56.149
I will

00:44:56.149 --> 00:44:58.169
remove the reason for those behaviors.

00:44:58.169 --> 00:45:00.200
Does that make sense?

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:02.639
That's behavioral assessment and treatment.

00:45:02.639 --> 00:45:04.829
Thinking about what causes it,

00:45:04.829 --> 00:45:06.320
altering the contingencies

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:10.430
so the behavior is no longer functional-- there's no reason to scream
"the Rosenberg findout";

00:45:10.430 --> 00:45:11.729
work's not hard--

00:45:11.729 --> 00:45:12.960
or if work's hard,

00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:13.690
I raise my hand,

00:45:13.690 --> 00:45:14.799
you help me,

00:45:14.799 --> 00:45:16.529
and I say, "Whoa,

00:45:16.529 --> 00:45:17.769
that worked."

00:45:17.769 --> 00:45:20.769
I'm going to raise my hand when I need help; the guy comes over. Ok."

00:45:20.769 --> 00:45:21.950
That's a lot easier

00:45:21.950 --> 00:45:25.599
than running around the room and having people upset with you.

00:45:25.599 --> 00:45:28.029
When we do behavioral assessment and treatment,	

00:45:28.029 --> 00:45:31.369
we're going to first think of changing the
environment. 

00:45:31.369 --> 00:45:34.329
We look at the target behavior. We say,

00:45:34.329 --> 00:45:38.689
"Hm, got this guy sitting at the table with four
other kids, four other students.

00:45:38.689 --> 00:45:41.469
What's one environmental modification I can make

00:45:41.469 --> 00:45:44.459
just to the physical environment?

00:45:44.459 --> 00:45:47.059
What comes to mind quickly?

00:45:47.059 --> 00:45:50.789
Four kids at the table.

00:45:50.789 --> 00:45:52.869
Let him work at his own desk. I could reduce

00:45:52.869 --> 00:45:55.669
the crowding.

00:45:55.669 --> 00:45:57.689
For anybody who's done any basic

00:45:57.689 --> 00:45:58.830
animal research:

00:45:58.830 --> 00:46:01.029
what happens with crowding?

00:46:01.029 --> 00:46:04.849
What does it generally do to arousal rates?

00:46:04.849 --> 00:46:07.589
What does it generally do to stereotypic responses?

00:46:07.589 --> 00:46:10.680
It ramps them up, right?

00:46:10.680 --> 00:46:13.489
I mean, for that matter it also increases cortisone levels. So

00:46:13.489 --> 00:46:17.939
you've got poor response sets, you've got poor adaptive
responses in

00:46:17.939 --> 00:46:21.519
highly crowded situations. Well, most of us
would say, well, four kids at a big table, 

00:46:21.519 --> 00:46:23.589
not so crowded. But

00:46:23.589 --> 00:46:28.429
what if you are overstimulated by physical
movement and you see people moving and you

00:46:28.429 --> 00:46:30.689
watch people move, and it distracts you

00:46:30.689 --> 00:46:34.009
from your task and you can't get back onto
your task because

00:46:34.009 --> 00:46:35.559
people are moving around

00:46:35.559 --> 00:46:38.579
and it's an unpredictable visual field for you.

00:46:38.579 --> 00:46:40.789
There is a problem that can be an aversive event

00:46:40.789 --> 00:46:41.759
in autism--

00:46:41.759 --> 00:46:44.099
might not be for you or me,

00:46:44.099 --> 00:46:45.670
but it could be for someone in autism.

00:46:45.670 --> 00:46:47.569
The second thing that you do

00:46:47.569 --> 00:46:52.489
is you manipulate contingencies. Now the contingencies
we talk about in this particular case are

00:46:52.489 --> 00:46:53.640
escape from demand

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:55.279
and positive reinforcement.

00:46:55.279 --> 00:46:58.509
So we want to do something to tinker with those. We want to

00:46:58.509 --> 00:47:00.410
change the escape

00:47:00.410 --> 00:47:01.999
consequence.

00:47:01.999 --> 00:47:05.750
And we want to turn the escape consequence into 
a positive behavior

00:47:05.750 --> 00:47:07.770
that gets what he needs, which

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:08.920
might be

00:47:08.920 --> 00:47:10.619
assistance when he raises his hand

00:47:10.619 --> 00:47:11.740
as opposed to

00:47:11.740 --> 00:47:15.239
no assistance, return to work when he
flips the table.

00:47:15.239 --> 00:47:19.119
Because right now, flip the table means work
is gone, right?

00:47:19.119 --> 00:47:22.749
That's what you said the reinforcing contingency was before,
and you were absolutely right.

00:47:22.749 --> 00:47:24.519
So now we want to say, you flip the table,

00:47:24.519 --> 00:47:26.239
you do the work again.

00:47:26.239 --> 00:47:29.619
Instead of, flip the table and the work goes away.
That's reversing a contingency.

00:47:29.619 --> 00:47:31.570
We also want to say, "Raise your hand, 

00:47:31.570 --> 00:47:33.630
I help you with the work, make it easier."

00:47:33.630 --> 00:47:36.970
That's adding a positive contingency for a more adaptive,

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:40.019
and I would say adaptive and functional behavior.

00:47:40.019 --> 00:47:44.649
We teach a functionally equivalent alternative
response.

00:47:44.649 --> 00:47:49.839
You identified what it was. You said, "Raise his hand."
That's a very common one to use.

00:47:49.839 --> 00:47:54.159
And then finally we want to teach a behavior that
will address long-term needs, and the reason

00:47:54.159 --> 00:47:58.179
I use the Rosenberg findout is it basically
helps us wrap all this up.

00:47:58.179 --> 00:48:00.950
Because here you guys are, right, in your

00:48:00.950 --> 00:48:03.890
thirteenth, fourteenth, fifteenth year of schooling

00:48:03.890 --> 00:48:06.259
and you still remember how to raise your hands.

00:48:06.259 --> 00:48:10.279
Ok, and sometime you're going to be working someplace
in a large meeting,

00:48:10.279 --> 00:48:11.479
as a professional,

00:48:11.479 --> 00:48:14.369
and you're going to raise your hand.

00:48:14.369 --> 00:48:15.090
Hand-raising

00:48:15.090 --> 00:48:17.319
to solicit attention or assistance

00:48:17.319 --> 00:48:19.440
has longitudinal value,

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:20.789
and in this culture--

00:48:20.789 --> 00:48:22.379
most cultures, many cultures--

00:48:22.379 --> 00:48:23.860
it's a socially acceptable way of doing it. 

00:48:23.860 --> 00:48:25.569
People are going to pay attention.

00:48:25.569 --> 00:48:27.259
They give you attention,

00:48:27.259 --> 00:48:28.849
they reinforce your hand-raising. 

00:48:28.849 --> 00:48:34.559
The natural consequences are fairly evident. Now,

00:48:34.559 --> 00:48:35.839
skip what's written, 'cause

00:48:35.839 --> 00:48:38.619
there's a lot of fifty-cent words in here. 

00:48:38.619 --> 00:48:40.169
Here's the basic concept.

00:48:40.169 --> 00:48:42.769
You guys had the flu.

00:48:42.769 --> 00:48:44.239
You didn't feel so good,

00:48:44.239 --> 00:48:46.900
alright? Maybe some of you are sitting here
saying,

00:48:46.900 --> 00:48:48.309
"I didn't have lunch today.

00:48:48.309 --> 00:48:50.820
I'm really having a hard time paying
attention." Or

00:48:50.820 --> 00:48:52.079
"I not only had lunch, 

00:48:52.079 --> 00:48:53.729
I had four desserts,

00:48:53.729 --> 00:48:58.669
and I'm about to hit a hypoglycemic low, and it's gonna get ugly."

00:48:58.669 --> 00:49:00.119
That is a setting event,

00:49:00.119 --> 00:49:05.420
alright? It is not an immediate trigger stimulus,
it's not the immediate antecedent. 

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:10.299
It is what occurs before that that sets the stage

00:49:10.299 --> 00:49:13.929
for the likelihood that reinforcement will
occur or the likelihood that punishment will

00:49:13.929 --> 00:49:14.549
occur.

00:49:14.549 --> 00:49:17.390
And that's why we call it a motivating operation,
because it

00:49:17.390 --> 00:49:21.460
establishes or it sets the stage for
the next operation-- it could be a reinforcing or

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:26.869
punishing operation. When we say operation we're basically using 
the same word as contingency. 

00:49:26.869 --> 00:49:31.319
The easy ones we think about are, "So how are you
feeling? Are you hungry, are you tired, have you had

00:49:31.319 --> 00:49:35.849
the wrong foods to eat?" Some of you guys had eight
cups of coffee today, some of you had

00:49:35.849 --> 00:49:36.959
maybe none,

00:49:36.959 --> 00:49:40.970
alright, and your caffeine levels are a little
better.

00:49:40.970 --> 00:49:44.929
Jamie and I were talking before. I just came
back from working in Italy 

00:49:44.929 --> 00:49:49.279
where we have three projects going on, and one of things
that I get to do in Italy is I get to drink

00:49:49.279 --> 00:49:52.179
espresso the way you're supposed to drink espresso, 

00:49:52.179 --> 00:49:53.009
but I don't drink

00:49:53.009 --> 00:49:54.939
caff  americano, I don't drink a 

00:49:54.939 --> 00:49:56.170
big cup of coffee.

00:49:56.170 --> 00:49:58.910
Basically I'm doing shots. 

00:49:58.910 --> 00:50:01.900
But I'm somebody who really likes coffee a lot--

00:50:01.900 --> 00:50:04.109
not a little; a lot-- and

00:50:04.109 --> 00:50:07.929
I drink a lot of espresso and it doesn't
affect me to have

00:50:07.929 --> 00:50:08.750
twelve, fifteen

00:50:08.750 --> 00:50:11.759
espressos a day.

00:50:11.759 --> 00:50:15.079
It's kind of an interesting thing. But if I were
to have the equivalent amount of caffeine

00:50:15.079 --> 00:50:17.449
in coffee here, I'd be non-functional. 

00:50:17.449 --> 00:50:21.979
I think it's-- I think it's a volume--
and the short burst that goes through. I just sort

00:50:21.979 --> 00:50:26.400
of keep on a constant fuel cycle with it.

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:28.129
Hey, for better or for worse. It--

00:50:28.129 --> 00:50:32.359
I could show you this exact same lecture done
on fifteen espressos in front of,

00:50:32.359 --> 00:50:35.299
you know, an audience of three hundred people
Italy with

00:50:35.299 --> 00:50:38.539
simultaneous translation and I probably wouldn't
be talking any differently. 

00:50:38.539 --> 00:50:41.099
It's just one of those things. But the reality is
that

00:50:41.099 --> 00:50:45.979
some of these things set the stage for better
or poor performance, and they are

00:50:45.979 --> 00:50:47.499
internal events, ok?

00:50:47.499 --> 00:50:49.469
Some of them are external events.

00:50:49.469 --> 00:50:51.549
Some of you guys working at Benhaven

00:50:51.549 --> 00:50:52.770
have clients

00:50:52.770 --> 00:50:53.680
that you work with

00:50:53.680 --> 00:50:59.449
who have schedules of their activities, right? Picture
schedules? No? Ok. 

00:50:59.449 --> 00:51:00.849
Let's say that

00:51:00.849 --> 00:51:01.780
you're working--

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:04.249
are you in the classroom?

00:51:04.249 --> 00:51:05.489
You're in the residence?

00:51:05.489 --> 00:51:07.790
But there's a schedule in residence for activities,
right?

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:10.189
Let's just say you go in there and say, "Hey, you know what?

00:51:10.189 --> 00:51:10.980
I'm smart.

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:12.680
I'm in molecular biology"--

00:51:12.680 --> 00:51:16.089
What are you in? What's your field?

00:51:16.089 --> 00:51:17.700
Biochem. Alright. "I'm in biochem.

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:20.769
I know more than these psychology geeks who are doing
this autism stuff. 

00:51:20.769 --> 00:51:24.009
We're doing away with the schedules."

00:51:24.009 --> 00:51:27.519
And behaviors go off the wall.

00:51:27.519 --> 00:51:30.409
Now, what's the relationship between schedules and
behaviors going off the wall,

00:51:30.409 --> 00:51:35.039
for a bunch of people with autism?

00:51:35.039 --> 00:51:39.630
Why would they have anxiety?

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:41.529
What does the schedule give them?

00:51:41.529 --> 00:51:45.569
You're right on the mark here. What does the schedule give 'em? Hm?

00:51:45.569 --> 00:51:49.939
A sense of control, and I don't mean coercive control
or manipulative control, but sort of

00:51:49.939 --> 00:51:51.469
knowing what's next. 

00:51:51.469 --> 00:51:54.269
Let's say structure, routine, predictability.

00:51:54.269 --> 00:51:55.140
Schedules give you that.

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:57.719
Some of the people have

00:51:57.719 --> 00:51:58.869
PDAs, you've got

00:51:58.869 --> 00:52:00.069
other kinds of things.

00:52:00.069 --> 00:52:01.390
I'm more basic,

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:03.299
I have this.

00:52:03.299 --> 00:52:05.489
And I make lists.

00:52:05.489 --> 00:52:09.369
Any of you make lists, nobody here? Do you?

00:52:09.369 --> 00:52:12.809
It's not a twelve-step program, it's just-- any of you make lists?

00:52:12.809 --> 00:52:16.219
What do you do with your lists when you finish stuff?

00:52:16.219 --> 00:52:19.579
Why do you cross 'em off?

00:52:19.579 --> 00:52:21.669
Because it feels good?

00:52:21.669 --> 00:52:24.599
Ok, good. Well, at least there's honesty here.

00:52:24.599 --> 00:52:29.539
And now-- you cross them off because it feels good.
Do you cross it off because you finished it? 

00:52:29.539 --> 00:52:32.579
Ok, it's not about the work, it's how I feel, ok.

00:52:32.579 --> 00:52:35.180
So you cross it off because you finished it,
and

00:52:35.180 --> 00:52:42.180
as your list gets crossed off, what are you
doing?

00:52:42.279 --> 00:52:44.249
You're completing your tasks. 

00:52:44.249 --> 00:52:45.130
As the

00:52:45.130 --> 00:52:47.280
number of cross-offs increases,

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:50.579
and the uncrossed off items decreases,

00:52:50.579 --> 00:52:53.559
what do we call that?

00:52:53.559 --> 00:52:57.489
Progress, right? We call it-- it's a progression of time and
task.

00:52:57.489 --> 00:52:59.630
If you have a schedule for a person with autism,

00:52:59.630 --> 00:53:02.709
and they're starting here on the schedule
and they're working their way down, and as they

00:53:02.709 --> 00:53:05.839
complete the activity they take the picture way
or the words away that 

00:53:05.839 --> 00:53:08.099
identified that particular task, 

00:53:08.099 --> 00:53:12.009
they're doing exactly the same thing.

00:53:12.009 --> 00:53:15.329
Now, I don't want any hands for this, but some of you guys, when
you make these lists,

00:53:15.329 --> 00:53:17.819
because you're gonna across things off so you can feel
good about it--

00:53:17.819 --> 00:53:19.890
you put things on the list you've already done. 

00:53:19.890 --> 00:53:22.859
So you cross them off right away.

00:53:22.859 --> 00:53:24.049
There's about

00:53:24.049 --> 00:53:27.439
a quarter of you in here that do that. It's not 
because you're bad people. 

00:53:27.439 --> 00:53:31.089
You just need a lot more reinforcement to
motivate yourself. And it's really ok to

00:53:31.089 --> 00:53:32.259
do that.

00:53:32.259 --> 00:53:35.569
You have my permission to do that. 

00:53:35.569 --> 00:53:38.719
When you have a behavior issue,

00:53:38.719 --> 00:53:41.559
a lot of times people say, "Well, what was the
immediate trigger?"

00:53:41.559 --> 00:53:46.089
You'll only get so far if you think of those immediate triggers.
You have to move up a level

00:53:46.089 --> 00:53:48.759
to ask what's the physical context (think crowding);

00:53:48.759 --> 00:53:51.239
what's the social context

00:53:51.239 --> 00:53:53.589
(think sarcasm--

00:53:53.589 --> 00:53:55.039
tell you about that in just a second);

00:53:55.039 --> 00:54:01.189
what's the biological context (think illness). Ok? So. Let's see.

00:54:01.189 --> 00:54:06.729
We'll pick somebody here that I haven't picked on yet. 

00:54:06.729 --> 00:54:11.220
Lady in the back who's really typing madly on there. I 
could say, 'cause you have a pendant on 

00:54:11.220 --> 00:54:12.449
that's shaped like a heart--

00:54:12.449 --> 00:54:13.570
I could say,

00:54:13.570 --> 00:54:15.569
"Wow, beautiful pendant!" Right?

00:54:15.569 --> 00:54:16.489
Or I could go,

00:54:16.489 --> 00:54:18.640
"Beautiful pendant."

00:54:18.640 --> 00:54:19.949
Same words. 

00:54:19.949 --> 00:54:25.499
What differs?

00:54:25.499 --> 00:54:29.069
Tone, good. What else?

00:54:29.069 --> 00:54:32.099
Affect, good. What else?

00:54:32.099 --> 00:54:33.249
Don't think words,

00:54:33.249 --> 00:54:39.159
think face. Yeah. Well, facial 

00:54:39.159 --> 00:54:40.410
expression, sure.

00:54:40.410 --> 00:54:44.099
One was a little friendlier, one was a little

00:54:44.099 --> 00:54:46.239
not-so-friendlier. 

00:54:46.239 --> 00:54:51.999
What's the most important piece of information? The words?

00:54:51.999 --> 00:54:53.089
No. Not the words.

00:54:53.089 --> 00:54:55.089
Because the words are the same. 

00:54:55.089 --> 00:54:57.609
Tone, facial expression,

00:54:57.609 --> 00:54:59.169
affect,

00:54:59.169 --> 00:55:00.869
volume, voice

00:55:00.869 --> 00:55:03.380
all of that comes together

00:55:03.380 --> 00:55:05.159
as social context

00:55:05.159 --> 00:55:09.640
to say, "In one, he was giving a compliment, but
the other, maybe he was being sarcastic.

00:55:09.640 --> 00:55:13.329
Wouldn't that be rude, for some guy you've never seen
before to walk into your class and be sarcastic

00:55:13.329 --> 00:55:15.339
about your pendant?"

00:55:15.339 --> 00:55:19.069
You might go on to think all
kinds of things about what a not nice or

00:55:19.069 --> 00:55:22.219
nice person I might be in other situations,

00:55:22.219 --> 00:55:22.720
depending on

00:55:22.720 --> 00:55:23.999
how I give you

00:55:23.999 --> 00:55:25.849
either one of those two responses.

00:55:25.849 --> 00:55:28.419
That's context.

00:55:28.419 --> 00:55:30.309
Now, that informs

00:55:30.309 --> 00:55:34.069
any of these, and you need to know what these
are

00:55:34.069 --> 00:55:38.469
or you're going to short yourself on understanding
this. You need the background. 

00:55:38.469 --> 00:55:39.869
So if you think about

00:55:39.869 --> 00:55:43.499
what's going on in the macro, and we think about
this as more macro, and now what's going on

00:55:43.499 --> 00:55:44.579
in the micro,

00:55:44.579 --> 00:55:46.959
the immediate trigger that sets it off--

00:55:46.959 --> 00:55:48.759
you have a better understanding

00:55:48.759 --> 00:55:52.160
of the experience of the person with an autism
spectrum disorder

00:55:52.160 --> 00:55:55.099
in that behavioral moment.

00:55:55.099 --> 00:55:58.519
Now in behavioral assessment we do four things, and I'm
going to tell you about them and then we'll go through them.

00:55:58.519 --> 00:56:00.439
I have some videos as we go through these too.

00:56:00.439 --> 00:56:04.969
An ecological assessment, a motivational assessment,
reinforcer, and then a functional assessment or 

00:56:04.969 --> 00:56:06.349
functional analysis. 

00:56:06.349 --> 00:56:08.969
Is anybody in here--

00:56:08.969 --> 00:56:13.089
has anybody in here been involved in doing functional
analysis before?

00:56:13.089 --> 00:56:16.579
As in a contingency reversal,

00:56:16.579 --> 00:56:18.829
or came from behavioral 

00:56:18.829 --> 00:56:20.209
background anywhere else or

00:56:20.209 --> 00:56:22.019
is moving towards that? Ok. 

00:56:22.019 --> 00:56:27.509
Or work with little autistic kids where you did
ABA or anything along those lines? No? You did? Ok.

00:56:27.509 --> 00:56:30.389
As a home tutor?

00:56:30.389 --> 00:56:32.529
It's a summer camp. Ok.

00:56:32.529 --> 00:56:36.150
Functional assessment and functional analysis are some
of the things we get really involved with

00:56:36.150 --> 00:56:40.349
to understand learning as well as behavioral
issues.

00:56:40.349 --> 00:56:41.950
We talk about ecological assessment--

00:56:41.950 --> 00:56:43.540
I want you to think macro,

00:56:43.540 --> 00:56:46.180
what's the big picture telling us? We do several
things.

00:56:46.180 --> 00:56:49.720
We look at the physical environment. We say,
"What in this physical environment might set

00:56:49.720 --> 00:56:50.630
the stage?"

00:56:50.630 --> 00:56:53.139
With the Rosenberg findout, we said, "Where is the kid sitting?

00:56:53.139 --> 00:56:54.459
Is it crowded?

00:56:54.459 --> 00:56:56.410
Is it noisy?"

00:56:56.410 --> 00:56:58.429
What if I told you that instead of

00:56:58.429 --> 00:57:00.449
the paper that I brought

00:57:00.449 --> 00:57:02.109
to the kid that was open-ended

00:57:02.109 --> 00:57:03.139
triggering him,

00:57:03.139 --> 00:57:05.209
it was his classmate

00:57:05.209 --> 00:57:06.599
who hums

00:57:06.599 --> 00:57:08.900
coming into the room, sitting next to him,

00:57:08.900 --> 00:57:10.070
and he's constantly humming.

00:57:10.070 --> 00:57:12.109
And that's the trigger.

00:57:12.109 --> 00:57:15.089
Whenever he hears the hum, he flips the table.

00:57:15.089 --> 00:57:16.889
There'd be a different intervention.

00:57:16.889 --> 00:57:19.299
One of the interventions that you might suggest
would be,

00:57:19.299 --> 00:57:22.940
"How about we move the humming guy away from the
guy who's flipping the tables?"

00:57:22.940 --> 00:57:23.959
Or

00:57:23.959 --> 00:57:28.559
what else could you do to the guy who has to
listen to the hum?

00:57:28.559 --> 00:57:35.559
To soften the impact of the hum.

00:57:37.529 --> 00:57:40.009
Huh? Yeah, ear buds. Or headphones, for something that

00:57:40.009 --> 00:57:42.060
kind of puts white noise behind it, drowns it down. Ok. 

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:45.559
The next thing we look at are antecedent-behavior-consequent conditions

00:57:45.559 --> 00:57:48.789
Just call them ABC's of behavior, ok? And we're looking
to see,

00:57:48.789 --> 00:57:49.869
does the behavior

00:57:49.869 --> 00:57:54.309
have a reliable or predictable set of things
that almost always trigger it?

00:57:54.309 --> 00:57:58.539
Does the behavior itself look fairly consistent
in its presentation? And what are the typical consequences

00:57:58.539 --> 00:57:59.880
that occur?

00:57:59.880 --> 00:58:01.610
When we do an ecological assessment, 

00:58:01.610 --> 00:58:04.199
we often find that there are very reliable antecedents

00:58:04.199 --> 00:58:06.599
and very reliable consequences.

00:58:06.599 --> 00:58:09.339
'Cause behavior, believe it or not, is fairly predictable.

00:58:09.339 --> 00:58:13.489
Most people do things the same way over and
over again. They just don't think about that.

00:58:13.489 --> 00:58:16.669
The next thing we do is look at the learning
environment. In the case of the Rosenberg

00:58:16.669 --> 00:58:17.619
findout kid,

00:58:17.619 --> 00:58:19.260
that learning environment involved

00:58:19.260 --> 00:58:23.639
a cognitive task, being able to process open-ended
information,

00:58:23.639 --> 00:58:24.510
construct a rubric

00:58:24.510 --> 00:58:25.390
for producing

00:58:25.390 --> 00:58:27.969
a graphomotor response. 

00:58:27.969 --> 00:58:30.159
Fairly complex if you think about it in those terms.

00:58:30.159 --> 00:58:32.089
For this guy, he may have been able to read it

00:58:32.089 --> 00:58:33.070
and talk it,

00:58:33.070 --> 00:58:34.699
but not write it.

00:58:34.699 --> 00:58:37.849
You work with somebody with Asperger's syndrome,
the problem of

00:58:37.849 --> 00:58:39.219
being able to

00:58:39.219 --> 00:58:40.019
speak

00:58:40.019 --> 00:58:42.450
an amazingly coherent paragraph,

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:45.009
but not write more than three sentences,

00:58:45.009 --> 00:58:47.259
would not surprise a lot of us.

00:58:47.259 --> 00:58:52.039
Ok? 'Cause in Asperger's syndrome, a graphomotor deficit
thatcould be part of a right hemisphere

00:58:52.039 --> 00:58:52.560
deficiency

00:58:52.560 --> 00:58:54.540
is a very common thing to have happen.

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:57.289
And then finally a temporal analysis has to do
with,

00:58:57.289 --> 00:58:58.670
"When does this occur? Early in the day,

00:58:58.670 --> 00:59:02.099
after lunch, right before lunch?

00:59:02.099 --> 00:59:04.769
Only on Mondays?

00:59:04.769 --> 00:59:07.750
Only on Wednesday mornings but never any
other day of the week?"

00:59:07.750 --> 00:59:10.019
And then you go back and do a little looking, 

00:59:10.019 --> 00:59:13.309
and you find out, Wow,

00:59:13.309 --> 00:59:15.869
mom and dad don't live together--

00:59:15.869 --> 00:59:19.799
no, mom and dad do live together, he's got an intact
family, but every Tuesday night he goes and stays 

00:59:19.799 --> 00:59:21.519
with Nanny and Grampy.

00:59:21.519 --> 00:59:23.589
And Nanny and Grampy are

00:59:23.589 --> 00:59:24.469
nannies and grampies.

00:59:24.469 --> 00:59:26.499
They do whatever he wants,

00:59:26.499 --> 00:59:28.849
no contingencies. 

00:59:28.849 --> 00:59:29.959
It's a free-for-all.

00:59:29.959 --> 00:59:31.549
The kid comes in the next morning thinking,

00:59:31.549 --> 00:59:32.859
"I can get whatever I want.

00:59:32.859 --> 00:59:34.509
I just did."

00:59:34.509 --> 00:59:36.289
So what we have now

00:59:36.289 --> 00:59:39.159
is the infamous nanny and grumpy interventions,

00:59:39.159 --> 00:59:40.519
which have

00:59:40.519 --> 00:59:42.769
probably more to do with fixing this behavior

00:59:42.769 --> 00:59:46.180
than working on the kid.

00:59:46.180 --> 00:59:50.199
When we have a motivational assessment, we're asking questions
about what makes this behavior go, what makes

00:59:50.199 --> 00:59:51.029
it happen.

00:59:51.029 --> 00:59:53.139
We have several things that we can think about.
The first

00:59:53.139 --> 00:59:55.210
that we described is positive reinforcement.

00:59:55.210 --> 00:59:56.709
It can be social attention

00:59:56.709 --> 00:59:58.099
or stuff.

00:59:58.099 --> 01:00:00.739
You are reinforced by what I give you.

01:00:00.739 --> 01:00:04.589
You really like these little kind of neat pointers, and then I give it to you.

01:00:04.589 --> 01:00:06.209
And then you play with it.

01:00:06.209 --> 01:00:08.889
You shine it at people, do things like that.

01:00:08.889 --> 01:00:11.809
That's a positive reinforcer or a material reinforcer. 

01:00:11.809 --> 01:00:14.329
Social attention, however, is all about us.

01:00:14.329 --> 01:00:16.160
You do things and I 

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:19.879
respond to you and you do more of that and
I respond more to you. 

01:00:19.879 --> 01:00:21.129
Escape/avoidance

01:00:21.129 --> 01:00:24.239
of demands is exactly what he did.

01:00:24.239 --> 01:00:28.439
Reinforcer loss is really a subset of this
one. I have it separated for a very important reason,

01:00:28.439 --> 01:00:29.389
because

01:00:29.389 --> 01:00:31.109
behavior is maintained

01:00:31.109 --> 01:00:34.400
by losing access, is what we're talking about. 

01:00:34.400 --> 01:00:35.430
So you're

01:00:35.430 --> 01:00:37.329
playing your Nintendo DS, 

01:00:37.329 --> 01:00:39.519
and you haven't hit the level yet

01:00:39.519 --> 01:00:41.929
and if you turn it off or close it up,

01:00:41.929 --> 01:00:44.159
you're gonna lose all your points thus far, right?

01:00:44.159 --> 01:00:47.630
That's what my seven-year-old tells me. He says, "I can't turn
it off and go start my homework

01:00:47.630 --> 01:00:49.339
because I'm between levels.

01:00:49.339 --> 01:00:50.460
And since I can't do the DS,

01:00:50.460 --> 01:00:53.179
I know I'm getting scammed, but I don't know
how to beat this problem.

01:00:53.179 --> 01:00:55.219
'Cause I know he really can

01:00:55.219 --> 01:00:58.029
to stop this and save it, butÉ

01:00:58.029 --> 01:01:00.319
It's a trick you play on your parents, I think. 

01:01:00.319 --> 01:01:02.439
They lose all the time. 

01:01:02.439 --> 01:01:04.359
The loss of access

01:01:04.359 --> 01:01:05.949
to something that you like,

01:01:05.949 --> 01:01:08.669
a material thing that you like. 

01:01:08.669 --> 01:01:11.439
Or you say, "I want it," and I say, "Not now."

01:01:11.439 --> 01:01:13.419
You have to wait; you can't have it now." That's 

01:01:13.419 --> 01:01:16.180
a problem that is about reinforcer loss.

01:01:16.180 --> 01:01:21.059
The next one has to do with sensory consequences, reinforcement
or negative reinforcement.

01:01:21.059 --> 01:01:25.199
Sensory reinforcement is...

01:01:25.199 --> 01:01:26.800
I am much more interested

01:01:26.800 --> 01:01:29.539
in the way this looks

01:01:29.539 --> 01:01:31.549
than in talking to you. 

01:01:31.549 --> 01:01:33.739
And I will do this for a really long time.

01:01:33.739 --> 01:01:36.589
It doesn't make any difference to me what you
think or what I think, 

01:01:36.589 --> 01:01:40.369
I like this; it's giving me what kind of feedback?

01:01:40.369 --> 01:01:42.439
Let me just tell you what I'm doing; I'm looking through my fingers and

01:01:42.439 --> 01:01:43.830
I'm looking at that light. 

01:01:43.830 --> 01:01:45.630
and I'm going like this.

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:51.190
What kind of feedback am I getting?

01:01:51.190 --> 01:01:54.789
Visual, good. What type of visual feedback?

01:01:54.789 --> 01:01:56.479
Remember: fingers, the light.

01:01:56.479 --> 01:01:58.139
I'm going to give you a clue:

01:01:58.139 --> 01:02:02.189
if I look at my fingers, if I focus on my
fingers,

01:02:02.189 --> 01:02:06.249
something different happens to the background
with the light. If I focus on the light, I have

01:02:06.249 --> 01:02:08.259
eight fingers.

01:02:08.259 --> 01:02:11.439
I'm getting a-- what?

01:02:11.439 --> 01:02:16.529
A little figure/ground distortion?

01:02:16.529 --> 01:02:22.099
You with me? Try it. I'm the guy on YouTube, you're not. 

01:02:22.099 --> 01:02:24.979
Alright. It's my life. You can do this in the privacy of your own home.

01:02:24.979 --> 01:02:28.019
I lied, you're all on YouTube, it's going to go up
in about a month.

01:02:28.019 --> 01:02:30.509
So you do this, 

01:02:30.509 --> 01:02:33.139
and I'm getting a figure/ground distortion.

01:02:33.139 --> 01:02:36.259
That's kind of close to 3-D, isn't it?

01:02:36.259 --> 01:02:39.660
Alright? See things in different dimensions, stereoscoped?

01:02:39.660 --> 01:02:43.659
If you're a five year old and you're on the playground
and you're doing this

01:02:43.659 --> 01:02:46.009
against the trees or against the leaves that are blowing

01:02:46.009 --> 01:02:47.919
on the trees,

01:02:47.919 --> 01:02:50.419
what do you think other five year olds are gonna do with me?

01:02:50.419 --> 01:02:52.349
Typical five year olds.

01:02:52.349 --> 01:02:53.229
Are they going to run up to you and say, "Wow,

01:02:53.229 --> 01:02:58.369
can I do that too?" You don't think? 

01:02:58.369 --> 01:03:00.659
Everybody makes fun of you. They might avoid you. 

01:03:00.659 --> 01:03:04.809
If they're five and they're nice, maybe they just won't even play with you. 

01:03:04.809 --> 01:03:06.929
They'll just say, "Hm," you know? By the time they get to seventh grade,

01:03:06.929 --> 01:03:09.989
that's when they make fun of you. 

01:03:09.989 --> 01:03:12.929
Now what can I go buy at a store right now. Toys R Us. 

01:03:12.929 --> 01:03:14.629
KB Toys. 

01:03:14.629 --> 01:03:16.629
What can I buy at a store

01:03:16.629 --> 01:03:18.069
that has been around for

01:03:18.069 --> 01:03:18.989
thirty years

01:03:18.989 --> 01:03:21.359
that provides really good

01:03:21.359 --> 01:03:23.709
figure/ground feedback

01:03:23.709 --> 01:03:25.059
that has

01:03:25.059 --> 01:03:25.469
context

01:03:25.469 --> 01:03:29.439
that other children would want to play with?

01:03:29.439 --> 01:03:34.639
A kaleidoscope, perfect. What else?

01:03:34.639 --> 01:03:41.639
The viewfinder thing. That's exactly
right, and it's-- yes, and it's a--

01:03:42.189 --> 01:03:44.029
you know what I'm talking about, right? A 

01:03:44.029 --> 01:03:48.179
viewmaster, thank you. I lost it for a second there. A viemaster is what you're thinking about. And I go and 

01:03:48.179 --> 01:03:49.070
buy a, you know, 

01:03:49.070 --> 01:03:51.949
Disney princess disc, put
it in there,

01:03:51.949 --> 01:03:55.469
and all the other little kids who like Disney
Princesses come over and they want to look

01:03:55.469 --> 01:03:55.900
at it.

01:03:55.900 --> 01:03:58.629
I do this, I generate avoidance behaviors. 

01:03:58.629 --> 01:04:00.510
I do a viewmaster

01:04:00.510 --> 01:04:03.549
with an interesting disc in it, and I generate

01:04:03.549 --> 01:04:05.439
approach behaviors.

01:04:05.439 --> 01:04:08.809
For the kid in the spectrum,

01:04:08.809 --> 01:04:09.559
both stimuli

01:04:09.559 --> 01:04:10.859
are providing

01:04:10.859 --> 01:04:12.179
similar

01:04:12.179 --> 01:04:14.769
visual perceptual feedback.

01:04:14.769 --> 01:04:18.089
Part of the intervention is often to understand

01:04:18.089 --> 01:04:20.789
what's maintaining the behavior at that level,

01:04:20.789 --> 01:04:24.309
at the sensory level, and saying, "Can I come up
with a better

01:04:24.309 --> 01:04:26.239
alternative that will access

01:04:26.239 --> 01:04:27.239
approach behaviors?"

01:04:27.239 --> 01:04:29.059
Part of your intervention is to

01:04:29.059 --> 01:04:32.679
bring them to the child, and still give the child things
that they're looking for.

01:04:32.679 --> 01:04:36.639
Classical conditioning certainly happens. Think
fears and phobias. And

01:04:36.639 --> 01:04:38.989
organic factors certainly occur. 

01:04:38.989 --> 01:04:43.289
We have a little guy right now-- we just finished
a sleep study down here,

01:04:43.289 --> 01:04:46.789
and he has obstructive sleep apnea.

01:04:46.789 --> 01:04:50.900
But nobody was thinking about that; they were just
thinking about this little kid who was tantruming wildly.

01:04:50.900 --> 01:04:51.650
Problem was,

01:04:51.650 --> 01:04:55.080
he was having so many episodes of obstructive
sleep apnea

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:56.929
that he was having no REM sleep.

01:04:56.929 --> 01:04:58.919
He would drop into REM, he would [snort].

01:04:58.919 --> 01:04:59.780
He would awake,

01:04:59.780 --> 01:05:02.580
he would snort and awake, and he'd pop right back
up again, and

01:05:02.580 --> 01:05:06.229
he basically couldn't recharge his batteries,
and this was going on for years.

01:05:06.229 --> 01:05:07.979
And he was a

01:05:07.979 --> 01:05:10.169
very unhappy little boy.

01:05:10.169 --> 01:05:12.689
And you know what the intervention was?

01:05:12.689 --> 01:05:17.909
Tonsils and adenoids. 

01:05:17.909 --> 01:05:19.030
The doc said,

01:05:19.030 --> 01:05:23.600
"We've got to cure him of obstructive sleep apnea, he's got tonsils
the size of oranges, and let's make those

01:05:23.600 --> 01:05:24.969
go away."

01:05:24.969 --> 01:05:25.830
And

01:05:25.830 --> 01:05:30.729
lo and behold, you have a physical intervention
for a physical problem that affects behavioral output. 

01:05:30.729 --> 01:05:32.869
You don't want to forget the fact that

01:05:32.869 --> 01:05:34.299
human beings sometimes have these

01:05:34.299 --> 01:05:36.709
events, physical events, that get in the way.

01:05:36.709 --> 01:05:40.349
We look at assessing stimulus preferences
as reinforcer assessment-- that's something

01:05:40.349 --> 01:05:44.639
we were doing when I was showing you the 

01:05:44.639 --> 01:05:48.469
the hand flapping episode and you came up
with an alternative, which was, get him a viewmaster,

01:05:48.469 --> 01:05:50.029
get him a kaleidoscope. 

01:05:50.029 --> 01:05:54.139
When you think about stimulus preferences, you say,
"What is the information the person is getting?"

01:05:54.139 --> 01:05:56.619
to guide what they're getting afterwards.

01:05:56.619 --> 01:05:59.690
Reinforcers that you pick have to be common and be available, right, 

01:05:59.690 --> 01:06:01.039
'cause if they're not--

01:06:01.039 --> 01:06:02.239
if they're exotic,

01:06:02.239 --> 01:06:04.209
they're not going to be in the natural environment. 

01:06:04.209 --> 01:06:06.309
So I may be able to teach you guys in this
class

01:06:06.309 --> 01:06:10.329
how to do something, because I have something
that you can't get anywhere else, 

01:06:10.329 --> 01:06:11.940
so by virtue of novelty,

01:06:11.940 --> 01:06:15.419
I shape your behavior. But if it doesn't work anywhere
else, it's not

01:06:15.419 --> 01:06:16.889
going to generalize the behavior,

01:06:16.889 --> 01:06:18.299
there'll be no fluency.

01:06:18.299 --> 01:06:21.679
Reinforcers have to be functional, meaning they work,
they increase behavior.

01:06:21.679 --> 01:06:26.089
And they really need to be age-appropriate. Now, 

01:06:26.089 --> 01:06:27.400
functional analysis

01:06:27.400 --> 01:06:28.719
is the process

01:06:28.719 --> 01:06:31.920
of answering the question in an empirical way,

01:06:31.920 --> 01:06:33.790
"Why does this behavior happen?"

01:06:33.790 --> 01:06:36.509
Ok? That's what this is all about.

01:06:36.509 --> 01:06:39.699
What I'm going to do is show you the four FAA

01:06:39.699 --> 01:06:40.929
vignettes

01:06:40.929 --> 01:06:41.859
at the end, 

01:06:41.859 --> 01:06:44.310
ok? If you want to stay for 'em, that's okay;
if you don't

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:47.789
I understand that.

01:06:47.789 --> 01:06:48.320
They're useful

01:06:48.320 --> 01:06:50.379
to the extent that they give you

01:06:50.379 --> 01:06:53.920
a real-life in-clinic

01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:58.019
observation of how we do it and what the
child's response is to that.

01:06:58.019 --> 01:07:01.420
But what do we get when we do a functional analysis? 
What we find out

01:07:01.420 --> 01:07:03.929
with empirical validation-- this is

01:07:03.929 --> 01:07:04.440
essentially

01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:06.230
having controlled conditions--

01:07:06.230 --> 01:07:08.040
it's a little experimentÉ Actually it's

01:07:08.040 --> 01:07:10.119
four little experiments. 

01:07:10.119 --> 01:07:13.890
You have a controlled condition where you do
something and get the response from the child.

01:07:13.890 --> 01:07:18.249
Or you don't do something and you see what the
response from the child is. 

01:07:18.249 --> 01:07:21.759
I look at this child with the Rosenberg findout's
behavior, and I say, 

01:07:21.759 --> 01:07:24.609
I don't allow him to escape--what happens to
the

01:07:24.609 --> 01:07:25.999
problem behavior?

01:07:25.999 --> 01:07:30.349
Up through the ceiling. In other words I say, "No,
just keep working on this worksheet."

01:07:30.349 --> 01:07:31.519
Or, as soon as he 

01:07:31.519 --> 01:07:33.389
starts to get fidgety, I say, "Hey, don't

01:07:33.389 --> 01:07:35.789
worry about the worksheet. Go take a break."

01:07:35.789 --> 01:07:38.059
The behavior ramps right back down again. 

01:07:38.059 --> 01:07:39.139
On, off,

01:07:39.139 --> 01:07:42.439
on, off. I want to watch the behavior rise and fall, rise and fall. 

01:07:42.439 --> 01:07:45.899
What I'm doing is experimentally validating the hypothesis. 

01:07:45.899 --> 01:07:49.679
The second thing we do is we find out what
consequences we're maintaining. 

01:07:49.679 --> 01:07:52.609
We're able to look at what we call chains of behavior.

01:07:52.609 --> 01:07:56.389
Ok? Now, I'll tell you what the chain was for this
guy--

01:07:56.389 --> 01:07:59.079
because, he'd be sitting in his chair, he'd be working kind of nicely,

01:07:59.079 --> 01:08:00.559
I'd bring his worksheet over, 

01:08:00.559 --> 01:08:02.479
put it down in front of him.

01:08:02.479 --> 01:08:03.869
I'd start to see him fidget,

01:08:03.869 --> 01:08:05.989
a little agitation,

01:08:05.989 --> 01:08:11.109
kind of like this, push the paper away...

01:08:11.109 --> 01:08:13.679
pop! Ok? Scream, jump, run. 

01:08:13.679 --> 01:08:16.009
If I wanna intervene at an early point

01:08:16.009 --> 01:08:20.359
in the behavioral chain, where do I go? 

01:08:20.359 --> 01:08:22.039
At what point do I intervene?

01:08:22.039 --> 01:08:27.589
Do I wait for him to scream, jump?

01:08:27.589 --> 01:08:29.869
Maybe when I saw him

01:08:29.869 --> 01:08:31.219
start to agitate, ok?

01:08:31.219 --> 01:08:33.170
And once the paper was down,

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:35.559
on a good day, he might do just fine.

01:08:35.559 --> 01:08:39.569
But if I start to see the agitation and I'm
saying to myself,

01:08:39.569 --> 01:08:43.180
"Predictably, reliably, that agitation is a precursor

01:08:43.180 --> 01:08:46.289
to the behavior of blowing up," and I 

01:08:46.289 --> 01:08:46.900
documented that--

01:08:46.900 --> 01:08:50.660
and how do I do that? I'll document that with
an ABC analysis. Then I'll say, "OK, 

01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:52.440
now I know where I'm going with this.

01:08:52.440 --> 01:08:55.479
If I intervene here with assistance,

01:08:55.479 --> 01:08:57.440
before he blows up,

01:08:57.440 --> 01:09:00.410
I'm able to capture the behavior, 

01:09:00.410 --> 01:09:02.049
respond to his need,

01:09:02.049 --> 01:09:03.539
before he has

01:09:03.539 --> 01:09:07.799
exhibited a problem behavior that I didn't have
to respond to for safety reasons, ok?

01:09:07.799 --> 01:09:12.900
We have high and low probability situations
that we find out when we do a functional assessment.

01:09:12.900 --> 01:09:16.659
We say, when is a behavior most likely to occur,
when is it least likely to occur?

01:09:16.659 --> 01:09:18.989
We look for functionally equivalent behavior--

01:09:18.989 --> 01:09:23.119
what's a thing we can teach them to do that
has the same communicative payoff, 

01:09:23.119 --> 01:09:25.429
but is more adaptive and successful?

01:09:25.429 --> 01:09:26.199
Raise your hand

01:09:26.199 --> 01:09:31.029
is more adaptive than flipping tables. 

01:09:31.029 --> 01:09:34.489
These are the conditions in a functional analysis. 

01:09:34.489 --> 01:09:36.530
And you'll see four of these conditions when--

01:09:36.530 --> 01:09:38.239
when I show you the tapes a little bit later.

01:09:38.239 --> 01:09:38.859
We've got

01:09:38.859 --> 01:09:42.770
a time when there's nothing going on, we don't
do anything to you. There's no consequence

01:09:42.770 --> 01:09:44.089
for your behavior. 

01:09:44.089 --> 01:09:51.079
We have a situation when the kid's playing by themselves, 
the kid engages in a problem behavior, alright? 

01:09:51.079 --> 01:09:52.689
And the adult responds to it.

01:09:52.689 --> 01:09:55.449
"Oh, don't worry; it's okay.

01:09:55.449 --> 01:09:57.020
Don't be concerned."

01:09:57.020 --> 01:10:00.810
But for appropriate behavior we don't do anything.
We only respond with social attention to

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:02.089
the problem behavior.

01:10:02.089 --> 01:10:05.809
Inn this situation, we get the problem behavior,
we give him something. 

01:10:05.809 --> 01:10:06.909
So the kid's 

01:10:06.909 --> 01:10:09.620
trying to get something; we put all the fun stuff
out of reach.

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:10.980
The kid says, "I want it." 

01:10:10.980 --> 01:10:14.590
The kid starts having a tantrum. We say, "Oh, here you go." 
And you give it to 'em. 

01:10:14.590 --> 01:10:19.120
Let him play with it for thirty seconds, then you put it away, put
it back up. The behavior ramps up again.

01:10:19.120 --> 01:10:21.319
"Ok, here you go." You give it back. 

01:10:21.319 --> 01:10:23.260
You manipulate his

01:10:23.260 --> 01:10:25.969
behavior with access to materials,

01:10:25.969 --> 01:10:28.090
and then later we're able to compare,

01:10:28.090 --> 01:10:32.270
does it get higher or lower when it's social
attention versus materials?

01:10:32.270 --> 01:10:33.819
See where this is going?

01:10:33.819 --> 01:10:35.760
We're able to experimentally validate

01:10:35.760 --> 01:10:37.909
in about

01:10:37.909 --> 01:10:39.260
two minutes

01:10:39.260 --> 01:10:40.449
for each of these, 

01:10:40.449 --> 01:10:42.389
times two or three--

01:10:42.389 --> 01:10:45.000
so we're talking about, what, five times two, 

01:10:45.000 --> 01:10:49.299
two, three, four, five times two is ten, times-- thirty
minutes, we can have

01:10:49.299 --> 01:10:52.589
an experimental validation

01:10:52.589 --> 01:10:53.699
of the effect

01:10:53.699 --> 01:10:55.459
of our attention,

01:10:55.459 --> 01:10:57.449
escape on demand,

01:10:57.449 --> 01:10:58.929
materials-- or

01:10:58.929 --> 01:11:02.780
nothing at all. The last couple conditions
have to do with, does behavior occur

01:11:02.780 --> 01:11:06.809
when there are no consequences going on. So
this one down here is

01:11:06.809 --> 01:11:08.799
designed to elicit stereotopy

01:11:08.799 --> 01:11:12.070
if it's going to occur-- in other words, he doesn't care about
the material or you.

01:11:12.070 --> 01:11:18.919
This one up here is essentially a baseline
condition, where it says "Free Play." 

01:11:18.919 --> 01:11:21.929
Ok, we're going to move past that for a second. 

01:11:21.929 --> 01:11:23.880
You could do several things to get this information.

01:11:23.880 --> 01:11:25.329
You can look at

01:11:25.329 --> 01:11:26.479
comments people make.

01:11:26.479 --> 01:11:27.619
You can review records.

01:11:27.619 --> 01:11:29.230
You can have interviews.

01:11:29.230 --> 01:11:33.449
You can do some fairly formal structured things. There are
several we use,

01:11:33.449 --> 01:11:35.410
they're available commercially.

01:11:35.410 --> 01:11:39.399
They're short and sweet. They're all based
on Likert scales--

01:11:39.399 --> 01:11:41.600
never happens, always happens, five points.

01:11:41.600 --> 01:11:47.050
With some degree of-- actually, a very good degree of
reliability and validity for each of these.

01:11:47.050 --> 01:11:52.010
The Motivation Assessment Scale and the Functional 
Analysis Screening Tool-- the MAS and the FAST--

01:11:52.010 --> 01:11:53.670
are the two most common ones used. 

01:11:53.670 --> 01:11:57.010
They have each twenty questions, you circle

01:11:57.010 --> 01:11:58.239
the items,

01:11:58.239 --> 01:12:00.719
the items are loaded

01:12:00.719 --> 01:12:01.420
for behavior

01:12:01.420 --> 01:12:02.780
that occurs

01:12:02.780 --> 01:12:04.150
because of attention

01:12:04.150 --> 01:12:05.719
versus escape from demand.

01:12:05.719 --> 01:12:08.819
So for example, on both of them there's a very similar
question:

01:12:08.819 --> 01:12:10.699
does this behavior occur

01:12:10.699 --> 01:12:12.509
in order to get your attention?

01:12:12.509 --> 01:12:14.400
Or does this behavior occur

01:12:14.400 --> 01:12:18.690
when you're in the room with other people and
you're talking to the other people, not the

01:12:18.690 --> 01:12:19.800
target child?

01:12:19.800 --> 01:12:21.749
And if you circle always,

01:12:21.749 --> 01:12:25.659
you're beginning to load towards social attention.

01:12:25.659 --> 01:12:27.409
Alternatively, there might be questions that say,

01:12:27.409 --> 01:12:29.550
does this behavior when the child's

01:12:29.550 --> 01:12:34.090
doing some-- when you give the child something difficult
to do,

01:12:34.090 --> 01:12:36.380
and won't take it away? 

01:12:36.380 --> 01:12:37.979
That's escape from demand.

01:12:37.979 --> 01:12:41.569
You have a number of questions for each of
these and they're loaded up into a little

01:12:41.569 --> 01:12:42.530
scoring algorithm,

01:12:42.530 --> 01:12:43.619
and they give you

01:12:43.619 --> 01:12:45.050
a hypothesis.

01:12:45.050 --> 01:12:47.159
They don't tell you the answer.

01:12:47.159 --> 01:12:49.829
Now why don't they tell you the answer?

01:12:49.829 --> 01:12:53.389
How are you gathering this information?

01:12:53.389 --> 01:12:55.170
Direct observation or

01:12:55.170 --> 01:12:57.260
talking to people?

01:12:57.260 --> 01:12:59.729
Talking to people. There's no direct observation
here.

01:12:59.729 --> 01:13:03.889
One of the things that we don't want to do
is limit our assessment to

01:13:03.889 --> 01:13:08.409
talking to people. We want to watch, see, and observe.

01:13:08.409 --> 01:13:12.469
When we get to a descriptive analysis, we're getting 
to the watch, see, and observe.

01:13:12.469 --> 01:13:17.010
An ABC analysis that I mentioned to you a little earlier,
something called a scatter plot--I'll show you both in a second.

01:13:17.010 --> 01:13:19.129
And then we can do direct observation.

01:13:19.129 --> 01:13:20.170
So if I was

01:13:20.170 --> 01:13:22.620
doing direct observation in this room of people

01:13:22.620 --> 01:13:23.849
who are

01:13:23.849 --> 01:13:25.360
looking at the screen,

01:13:25.360 --> 01:13:27.430
I could be watching the clock in the back

01:13:27.430 --> 01:13:29.389
and then I could say whenever the clock,

01:13:29.389 --> 01:13:30.820
hits one of the

01:13:30.820 --> 01:13:33.899
numbers, in other words, if you have a 5 second
interval,

01:13:33.899 --> 01:13:37.340
I'm talking to you guys, I'm looking at
the clock and as soon as it hits,

01:13:37.340 --> 01:13:41.339
I look around and I see, "Are you writing or
not?"

01:13:41.339 --> 01:13:43.489
Sorry, "Are you looking at the board or not?"

01:13:43.489 --> 01:13:47.459
And I just put a little plus or minus. And then it
hits the next number, and I take a look at you.

01:13:47.459 --> 01:13:51.270
For the intervening five seconds, I don't care what you do.

01:13:51.270 --> 01:13:53.140
But on that 5 second mark,

01:13:53.140 --> 01:13:57.239
I will watch to see for the occurrence or
the non-occurrence of your behavior.

01:13:57.239 --> 01:13:58.219
That's what we call

01:13:58.219 --> 01:13:59.519
a time sample,

01:13:59.519 --> 01:14:01.099
and it's

01:14:01.099 --> 01:14:03.740
one of several different ways of gathering

01:14:03.740 --> 01:14:04.750
objective information

01:14:04.750 --> 01:14:10.229
that we then aggregate form some sort of a behavioral
profile.

01:14:10.229 --> 01:14:13.589
We won't spend a lot of time on this or the
next one because I think they're fairly 

01:14:13.589 --> 01:14:15.120
self-evident.

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:19.489
when you have what's called an ABC analysis, you see the A, what happened before, you see the B,

01:14:19.489 --> 01:14:22.089
the behavior itself, and C for the consequence.

01:14:22.089 --> 01:14:24.519
And a little description down at the bottom.

01:14:24.519 --> 01:14:29.370
When you have an ABC analysis, you are taking one
real-time event,

01:14:29.370 --> 01:14:31.940
and writing it down.

01:14:31.940 --> 01:14:35.800
So one flip of the table with the Rosenberg
findout. Here we say,

01:14:35.800 --> 01:14:37.770
"The kid was sitting at the table

01:14:37.770 --> 01:14:42.650
doing his thing, he screamed 'The Rosenberg find out,' flipped the table and ran to the door."

01:14:42.650 --> 01:14:45.230
What were the antecedents? Well, time of day,

01:14:45.230 --> 01:14:47.039
who he was with, what the date was.

01:14:47.039 --> 01:14:49.280
He's sitting at the table, it's after lunch,

01:14:49.280 --> 01:14:52.350
I bring over

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:56.340
the Halloween worksheet, and I say, "What are you going to be for Halloween?" He's got to fill it out and write me five

01:14:56.340 --> 01:14:57.679
sentences about that.

01:14:57.679 --> 01:15:01.820
He flips the table. Then what do we do? What do we do?

01:15:01.820 --> 01:15:05.519
We tell him to go sit down in the beanbag chair. What does he do? He gets quiet.

01:15:05.519 --> 01:15:07.469
We do 10 of these.

01:15:07.469 --> 01:15:09.559
Then we look for patterns.

01:15:09.559 --> 01:15:14.000
If we do 10 of these and we look for patterns, we are going
to find patterns most likely in the antecedents.

01:15:14.000 --> 01:15:22.969
Antecedents and consequences are going to give us patterns.

01:15:22.969 --> 01:15:24.899
If you had to choose,

01:15:24.899 --> 01:15:28.969
should you intervene at the antecedent level or at the consequence level?

01:15:28.969 --> 01:15:35.260
Which one would you choose first?

01:15:35.260 --> 01:15:41.909
What do you think? Yeah?

01:15:41.909 --> 01:15:45.389
Why? At the antecedent level. Why?

01:15:45.389 --> 01:15:47.140
You'll get it before it happens, right?

01:15:47.140 --> 01:15:51.590
Sure. It's a good idea, when you can, to intervene at the antecedent level so you don't

01:15:51.590 --> 01:15:53.969
have to deal with consequent responses.

01:15:53.969 --> 01:15:55.430
Recognize, of course, that this is

01:15:55.430 --> 01:15:58.729
human nature and there's fallibility involved
all over the place.

01:15:58.729 --> 01:16:02.769
But you do want to be able to get to the behavior before it happens, and maybe get to the early

01:16:02.769 --> 01:16:07.309
part of that chain, like the fidgeting we were
talking about.

01:16:07.309 --> 01:16:08.780
A scatter plot is nothing more than

01:16:08.780 --> 01:16:11.070
putting information on a time series.

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:13.150
So when this behavior occurs, by day,

01:16:13.150 --> 01:16:17.919
by hours here. Instead of hours on
this side, I could have activities,

01:16:17.919 --> 01:16:21.559
but all we're doing is putting tick marks on here.

01:16:21.559 --> 01:16:23.389
Nothing more than a check.

01:16:23.389 --> 01:16:26.860
Were looking for aggregated volumes of

01:16:26.860 --> 01:16:29.789
behavioral events.

01:16:29.789 --> 01:16:31.170
When we do an FBA,

01:16:31.170 --> 01:16:32.730
we gather all this information,

01:16:32.730 --> 01:16:35.449
we pull it together in a way that has some coherence,

01:16:35.449 --> 01:16:36.769
and we do that

01:16:36.769 --> 01:16:39.639
so that we can say

01:16:39.639 --> 01:16:43.579
what's making sense peer to Now there's a downside
to this too, because it's fairly time-consuming.

01:16:43.579 --> 01:16:44.760
You may accidentally

01:16:44.760 --> 01:16:48.569
or intentionally reinforce problem behaviors because,

01:16:48.569 --> 01:16:50.320
remember, if the behavior is to --

01:16:50.320 --> 01:16:53.499
in the functional analysis, if the behavior is to gain escape,

01:16:53.499 --> 01:16:56.260
and you don't allow escape, you will increase behavior.

01:16:56.260 --> 01:16:56.860
All right?

01:16:56.860 --> 01:16:59.949
You make a judgment as to whether it's a safe thing
to do or not.

01:16:59.949 --> 01:17:04.170
There's training demands because you have to be
very valid, very reliable with this, but

01:17:04.170 --> 01:17:08.480
you will experimentally confirm or disconfirm a hypothesis. That is the power of doing

01:17:08.480 --> 01:17:10.469
a functional analysis.

01:17:10.469 --> 01:17:11.880
I'll tell you at our clinic,

01:17:11.880 --> 01:17:16.130
at our cent,r we have a specific clinic for the
assessment and treatment of severe challenging

01:17:16.130 --> 01:17:17.029
behavior.

01:17:17.029 --> 01:17:20.679
We don't do functional analyses on a regular basis.

01:17:20.679 --> 01:17:21.480
We do them, as it were, for

01:17:21.480 --> 01:17:23.569
the worst of the worst behaviors. 

01:17:23.569 --> 01:17:26.550
We'll do a functional assessment, which is basically to say,

01:17:26.550 --> 01:17:32.059
let's get the hypothesis and test it in situ,
in real life.

01:17:32.059 --> 01:17:35.619
It's a lot easier in the school, it's a lot easier
to convince people that's a reasonable place to

01:17:35.619 --> 01:17:36.269
go.

01:17:36.269 --> 01:17:40.849
But what you'll be able to do with a functional analysis is to demonstrate with a fair degree of reliability

01:17:40.849 --> 01:17:44.589
and validity what's going on.

01:17:44.589 --> 01:17:48.880
Now, you get all this information, 85% of your work, and now you've got to do something with it.

01:17:48.880 --> 01:17:50.010
So you have to link

01:17:50.010 --> 01:17:52.309
your intervention with a treatment plan.

01:17:52.309 --> 01:17:54.360
And the reason I show you this --

01:17:54.360 --> 01:17:58.000
this particular slide is is just to emphasize that

01:17:58.000 --> 01:18:01.449
for the particular function, you have a particular
treatment plan.

01:18:01.449 --> 01:18:04.530
And you may have two or three of these. You may have the behavior that's--

01:18:04.530 --> 01:18:06.289
as in the case I gave you,

01:18:06.289 --> 01:18:09.409
there's both escape and access to positive
reinforcement

01:18:09.409 --> 01:18:09.849
operating,

01:18:09.849 --> 01:18:12.019
and you've got to have two

01:18:12.019 --> 01:18:13.829
integrated components of the treatment plan.

01:18:13.829 --> 01:18:15.660
So you have to be thinking ahead to know

01:18:15.660 --> 01:18:18.800
how do they work together.

01:18:18.800 --> 01:18:22.069
And when you do this,

01:18:22.069 --> 01:18:24.829
I guess there's really three take-home messages.

01:18:24.829 --> 01:18:28.329
First one is that behavior is functional. Second is communication. The third one is --

01:18:28.329 --> 01:18:29.360
LAN you're doing

01:18:29.360 --> 01:18:31.829
behavior management, you're really doing teaching.

01:18:31.829 --> 01:18:33.429
You have to teach an alternative.

01:18:33.429 --> 01:18:35.880
If you don't teach an alternative,

01:18:35.880 --> 01:18:38.039
you're leaving the client with nothing.

01:18:38.039 --> 01:18:40.489
If you make behavior go away,

01:18:40.489 --> 01:18:44.460
in a practical sense, you're saying to the person with autism, "Figure it out on your own.

01:18:44.460 --> 01:18:47.780
Figure out what to do next." Now, it's a social learning disability, they're not likely to pick the right 

01:18:47.780 --> 01:18:52.459
social behavior to demonstrate for you.

01:18:52.459 --> 01:18:54.829
If it's based on function, not form,

01:18:54.829 --> 01:18:59.409
what it allows us to do is to say we have particular skill sets

01:18:59.409 --> 01:19:04.499
that may be linked together. Different behaviors
may be linked together by function.

01:19:04.499 --> 01:19:08.099
We have general rules for intervention that we
consider. The first is, function is preeminent

01:19:08.099 --> 01:19:09.549
To the second is,

01:19:09.549 --> 01:19:11.579
it's called the Fair Pair rule, which means

01:19:11.579 --> 01:19:13.809
if you take one away, you put one back.

01:19:13.809 --> 01:19:15.459
You always replace.

01:19:15.459 --> 01:19:16.750
If I teach you

01:19:16.750 --> 01:19:19.699
not to flip tables, I must teach you what to
do instead.

01:19:19.699 --> 01:19:21.420
And the third thing is --

01:19:21.420 --> 01:19:24.279
any of you who work with kids or have worked
with kids in the past,

01:19:24.279 --> 01:19:28.610
if you keep people busy and happily engaged, you'll have fewer behavior problems.

01:19:28.610 --> 01:19:29.969
So as long as we

01:19:29.969 --> 01:19:31.539
think ahead to

01:19:31.539 --> 01:19:33.800
functional engagement,

01:19:33.800 --> 01:19:35.530
replacement skills that we teach,

01:19:35.530 --> 01:19:39.579
and looking at the functional application
of those skills so we can evaluate them effectively,

01:19:39.579 --> 01:19:41.760
we're in good shape.

01:19:41.760 --> 01:19:45.859
If you have communication skill teaching strategies,
social teaching programs,

01:19:45.859 --> 01:19:49.310
a behavior support plan is integrated with that.

01:19:49.310 --> 01:19:53.820
As you were suggesting in the back when I
talked to you about the schedules,

01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:54.599
routine,

01:19:54.599 --> 01:19:57.669
structure, predictability, order,

01:19:57.669 --> 01:19:58.960
control --

01:19:58.960 --> 01:20:02.430
those are elements of stability for a person with autism.

01:20:02.430 --> 01:20:06.600
The more we provide each of those in the person's experience,

01:20:06.600 --> 01:20:08.419
the more --

01:20:08.419 --> 01:20:10.649
the more appropriate behavior we're going to see,

01:20:10.649 --> 01:20:14.289
but we're also going to see more likely demonstration
of the behaviors that have been taught.

01:20:14.289 --> 01:20:15.950
So if we teach someone,

01:20:15.950 --> 01:20:18.989
"Schedules guide your day,

01:20:18.989 --> 01:20:20.739
transitions can happen but

01:20:20.739 --> 01:20:23.600
we can describe them visually on a schedule.

01:20:23.600 --> 01:20:25.289
When you've got a schedule,

01:20:25.289 --> 01:20:27.679
you can handle the transition."

01:20:27.679 --> 01:20:30.250
That's one of the things we can do for a person with autism

01:20:30.250 --> 01:20:35.430
that makes for lower arousal rates -- you'll call
it anxiety, I'll call it lower arousal, the same

01:20:35.430 --> 01:20:38.249
concept

01:20:38.249 --> 01:20:40.360
is going on here. Conversely, when we have breakdown 

01:20:40.360 --> 01:20:42.670
in structure, predictability, order

01:20:42.670 --> 01:20:43.299
or control,

01:20:43.299 --> 01:20:45.779
we're likely to see a behavior problem.

01:20:45.779 --> 01:20:47.389
So if I said to you,

01:20:47.389 --> 01:20:51.829
we've just had a breakdown in all those things,
and now we've got this behavior going on--

01:20:51.829 --> 01:20:57.039
what's one of your first thoughts on intervention?

01:20:57.039 --> 01:20:58.819
What can you do

01:20:58.819 --> 01:21:00.490
to try to bring behavior

01:21:00.490 --> 01:21:04.309
back to a better form?

01:21:04.309 --> 01:21:09.050
You could increase the --? Structure?

01:21:09.050 --> 01:21:10.999
Predictability?

01:21:10.999 --> 01:21:13.249
Routine? Control? Order

01:21:13.249 --> 01:21:15.429
of the client's experience.

01:21:15.429 --> 01:21:21.099
Two simply bring them back to what they expect,
bring them back to what's predictable.

01:21:21.099 --> 01:21:22.929
If we look at adult behavior,

01:21:22.929 --> 01:21:25.900
in the example I gave you a little earlier
with sarcasm,

01:21:25.900 --> 01:21:29.850
if we look at the adult behavior and say, "Is
it consistent or not?"

01:21:29.850 --> 01:21:32.630
we often see the chinks in the armor of

01:21:32.630 --> 01:21:33.520
problem behavior

01:21:33.520 --> 01:21:37.369
that we create -- not the client creates -- that we create.

01:21:37.369 --> 01:21:41.480
We've got a guy who we are working with right
now who has very serious

01:21:41.480 --> 01:21:44.849
aggressions and property destruction -- he's
little, he's years old,

01:21:44.849 --> 01:21:47.429
but one of the reasons he does is he works
in a room --

01:21:47.429 --> 01:21:50.679
if you guys can see the room, the little space in the back? --

01:21:50.679 --> 01:21:52.090
it's smaller than that.

01:21:52.090 --> 01:21:52.980
And it has

01:21:52.980 --> 01:21:54.360
shelves and cabinets in it.

01:21:54.360 --> 01:21:58.030
And it's a little nine-year-old kid with two adults
in a room that's that size.

01:21:58.030 --> 01:21:59.760
Talk about crowding.

01:21:59.760 --> 01:22:02.329
Has no natural light, has only fluorescent light,

01:22:02.329 --> 01:22:04.199
and he can't get out the door.

01:22:04.199 --> 01:22:08.179
So we've got big behavior problems going on this kids' life right there, and

01:22:08.179 --> 01:22:10.820
one of the things we have to recognize is

01:22:10.820 --> 01:22:12.730
some of the circumstances that the

01:22:12.730 --> 01:22:13.900
program he's in

01:22:13.900 --> 01:22:15.929
may have created for him,

01:22:15.929 --> 01:22:17.630
ramp up the likelihood of that behavior.

01:22:17.630 --> 01:22:20.260
He simply can't move.

01:22:20.260 --> 01:22:21.969
He has no way out of that.

01:22:21.969 --> 01:22:26.249
We sometimes teach people various kinds of
strategies to compensate or to move beyond 

01:22:26.249 --> 01:22:27.329
the behavior,

01:22:27.329 --> 01:22:28.870
which involves self-management.

01:22:28.870 --> 01:22:31.030
Sometimes relaxation strategies,

01:22:31.030 --> 01:22:35.599
sometimes what we're going to call a personal time out.

01:22:35.599 --> 01:22:39.689
Now, as we think about our intervention plans, we
are necessarily going to think about four

01:22:39.689 --> 01:22:41.560
things. We have to change

01:22:41.560 --> 01:22:43.230
communication.

01:22:43.230 --> 01:22:46.780
We have to change what the client says and does,

01:22:46.780 --> 01:22:48.170
and, how they do it

01:22:48.170 --> 01:22:49.479
so it works better.

01:22:49.479 --> 01:22:51.599
That's called functional communication training,

01:22:51.599 --> 01:22:53.619
and we're going to teach a better 

01:22:53.619 --> 01:22:56.780
response. Now, the better response, as you suggested, was hand-raising

01:22:56.780 --> 01:22:58.210
instead of flipping the table.

01:22:58.210 --> 01:22:59.950
The second thing we're going to do

01:22:59.950 --> 01:23:00.770
is we're going to teach

01:23:00.770 --> 01:23:03.809
the replacement skill. Remember the Fair Pair rule?

01:23:03.809 --> 01:23:05.460
You have a new skill you want to teach,

01:23:05.460 --> 01:23:07.580
you have to use reinforcement strategies

01:23:07.580 --> 01:23:10.440
to build that skill -- so you're going to teach the child

01:23:10.440 --> 01:23:11.280
or the adult

01:23:11.280 --> 01:23:13.510
to do something different

01:23:13.510 --> 01:23:16.929
that more likely elicits the response that they
want.

01:23:16.929 --> 01:23:19.389
You're going to modify the conditions around the behavior,

01:23:19.389 --> 01:23:21.929
if they're tired you give them sleep, if they are hungry

01:23:21.929 --> 01:23:24.670
they get two have something to eat, or

01:23:24.670 --> 01:23:26.059
perhaps it's crowded,

01:23:26.059 --> 01:23:28.639
perhaps there is no schedule to follow, perhaps there is

01:23:28.639 --> 01:23:32.599
no other way of them knowing what's coming
next and you address that

01:23:32.599 --> 01:23:34.580
as an antecedent condition.

01:23:34.580 --> 01:23:37.690
And finally we recognize that there are going to be consequences,

01:23:37.690 --> 01:23:41.190
because not all behavior can be highly predictable in
anyone's life,

01:23:41.190 --> 01:23:44.070
and we recognize that our consequence strategies have to be 

01:23:44.070 --> 01:23:45.300
highly,

01:23:45.300 --> 01:23:47.140
highly socially valid, meaning

01:23:47.140 --> 01:23:48.720
that they must be

01:23:48.720 --> 01:23:50.079
accepted by the consumers

01:23:50.079 --> 01:23:51.429
in the environment.

01:23:51.429 --> 01:23:54.279
By the consumers, we don't just talk about teachers or parents,

01:23:54.279 --> 01:23:55.760
we talk about

01:23:55.760 --> 01:24:00.239
other people in the environment -- the social
environment of the school, a community,

01:24:00.239 --> 01:24:02.650
a neighborhood, whether this particular

01:24:02.650 --> 01:24:05.070
behavior itself is considered aberrant.

01:24:05.070 --> 01:24:08.949
Whether the intervention strategy's considered
acceptable, and and whether the outcomes we seek to

01:24:08.949 --> 01:24:12.170
achieve are considered socially acceptable,
socially valid.

01:24:12.170 --> 01:24:15.670
We have to use the least restrictive interventions that

01:24:15.670 --> 01:24:18.479
can be used, because to

01:24:18.479 --> 01:24:19.159
euphemistically

01:24:19.159 --> 01:24:22.269
kill a fly with a cannon is pointless.

01:24:22.269 --> 01:24:25.239
But you can have very heavy-handed approaches

01:24:25.239 --> 01:24:28.150
misused in dealing with problem behaviors.

01:24:28.150 --> 01:24:31.439
and what you end up with is reinforcing escape.

01:24:31.439 --> 01:24:33.150
Now if you come all the way back to what

01:24:33.150 --> 01:24:35.089
the autism spectrum is all about,

01:24:35.089 --> 01:24:36.280
we have a group of people

01:24:36.280 --> 01:24:38.040
with a social learning disability

01:24:38.040 --> 01:24:40.819
who have problems with social interpretation

01:24:40.819 --> 01:24:43.559
who are more likely to be confused by the social
environment

01:24:43.559 --> 01:24:46.810
So now in addition to their confusion we give them punishment contingencies

01:24:46.810 --> 01:24:49.349
so they want to escape more. 

01:24:49.349 --> 01:24:50.869
Makes no sense.

01:24:50.869 --> 01:24:52.920
So the use of punishment procedures

01:24:52.920 --> 01:24:54.569
is really something that we approach

01:24:54.569 --> 01:24:55.999
in a minimalist way,

01:24:55.999 --> 01:24:59.360
and as you suggested a little bit earlier, we go
after the antecedents.

01:24:59.360 --> 01:25:00.309
On the equation,

01:25:00.309 --> 01:25:03.149
the left-hand side of the equal sign is where
we're headed;

01:25:03.149 --> 01:25:06.609
we recognize that we have to deal with the
right hand side,

01:25:06.609 --> 01:25:10.479
but we recognize more that the left-hand side
of that equation-- the antecedent

01:25:10.479 --> 01:25:13.589
or the environmental conditions are gonna
give us more information and more strategies to

01:25:13.589 --> 01:25:15.989
move forward.

01:25:15.989 --> 01:25:17.969
When we talk about treatment planning

01:25:17.969 --> 01:25:20.559
and some of the things we're going to do with
treatment planning,

01:25:20.559 --> 01:25:23.789
we recognize that we have functional communication
training,

01:25:23.789 --> 01:25:26.259
where we teach someone an alternative.

01:25:26.259 --> 01:25:29.719
So in teaching this young man to raise his hand
instead of

01:25:29.719 --> 01:25:30.730
slipping tables,

01:25:30.730 --> 01:25:33.530
I come all the way back to the idea that I
have to give him a way

01:25:33.530 --> 01:25:36.389
of getting

01:25:36.389 --> 01:25:37.490
my attention

01:25:37.490 --> 01:25:41.200
when he needs it, before he flips the table. And
I'm gonna give him a hard task or

01:25:41.200 --> 01:25:44.530
something that requires him to write a written response, and I am going to
be there to coach him

01:25:44.530 --> 01:25:49.289
to raise his hand, and then I give him the attention and reduce his demand.

01:25:49.289 --> 01:25:54.129
We conduct that particular intervention much
in the same way as we would conduct a functional

01:25:54.129 --> 01:25:54.809
assessment

01:25:54.809 --> 01:25:55.970
for a behavioral problem,

01:25:55.970 --> 01:25:59.539
but it's simply a functional assessment of communication.

01:25:59.539 --> 01:26:03.670
When behavior is maintained by reinforcement,
whether it's positive reinforcement for social

01:26:03.670 --> 01:26:06.329
attention, or whether it's materials themselves,

01:26:06.329 --> 01:26:08.719
we give someone

01:26:08.719 --> 01:26:10.239
escape from

01:26:10.239 --> 01:26:12.800
that reinforcing stimulus. So if you're going to misbehave

01:26:12.800 --> 01:26:14.199
to get my attention

01:26:14.199 --> 01:26:16.469
like that little kid on the video,

01:26:16.469 --> 01:26:18.969
I'm not going to give you attention when you are misbehaving.

01:26:18.969 --> 01:26:23.889
But when am I going to give you the attention?

01:26:23.889 --> 01:26:25.210
When you are --?

01:26:25.210 --> 01:26:27.619
When you are not misbehaving, when you're not tantruming,

01:26:27.619 --> 01:26:31.539
that's when I'm going to give you plenty of attention.
So I want you to learn this contrast

01:26:31.539 --> 01:26:33.679
called behavioral contrast, where we say,

01:26:33.679 --> 01:26:35.110
from the child's perspective,

01:26:35.110 --> 01:26:39.659
"I behave this way, it's sweetness and light. I behave this way, there's no attention.

01:26:39.659 --> 01:26:41.349
This way, no attention. 

01:26:41.349 --> 01:26:44.459
Yes, no. I want the attention I will behave this
way."

01:26:44.459 --> 01:26:48.440
We get to apply reinforcement, in this case
a differential reinforcement procedure,

01:26:48.440 --> 01:26:53.739
to teach an alternative  behavior which might
be getting attention  in a more socially acceptable way.

01:26:53.739 --> 01:26:55.789
If the behavior's maintained by escape,

01:26:55.789 --> 01:27:00.120
we  absolutely identify that we have to not allow
escape to be successful.

01:27:00.120 --> 01:27:01.709
Because it will strengthen the behavior.

01:27:01.709 --> 01:27:03.489
So what we're going to  do alternatively

01:27:03.489 --> 01:27:04.790
is we're going to try to

01:27:04.790 --> 01:27:06.249
make the environment rich

01:27:06.249 --> 01:27:07.710
with information, so

01:27:07.710 --> 01:27:10.550
the child says, "There's no need to escape.

01:27:10.550 --> 01:27:12.629
I don't want to escape from this task,

01:27:12.629 --> 01:27:14.840
because I like being in the task too much.

01:27:14.840 --> 01:27:16.110
I get help,

01:27:16.110 --> 01:27:17.459
the task is fun,

01:27:17.459 --> 01:27:21.229
the materials are preferable to me."

01:27:21.229 --> 01:27:25.469
And something that provides a significant
reinforcement for staying in the game

01:27:25.469 --> 01:27:27.360
versus leaving the game.

01:27:27.360 --> 01:27:32.189
In the case of behavior that is maintained by loss
of access to reinforcers,

01:27:32.189 --> 01:27:36.800
we have --  access to reinforcement is available predictably
throughout the day

01:27:36.800 --> 01:27:39.199
and can be earned

01:27:39.199 --> 01:27:41.010
but is not freely accessible.

01:27:41.010 --> 01:27:44.040
We put it on what's called a contingency for time.

01:27:44.040 --> 01:27:45.290
And while we do that,

01:27:45.290 --> 01:27:47.719
we also recognize that we're going to teach

01:27:47.719 --> 01:27:48.949
children and adults

01:27:48.949 --> 01:27:51.409
to wait to get what they want.

01:27:51.409 --> 01:27:54.269
Just like we all have to wait for
things that we want --

01:27:54.269 --> 01:27:56.610
we can't have immediately in most situations --

01:27:56.610 --> 01:28:00.900
we're going to say  that we are going to teach
someone that waiting is a skill to be acquired.

01:28:00.900 --> 01:28:03.650
When behavior is maintained by sensory consequences

01:28:03.650 --> 01:28:07.639
that are escape from a sensory event that is subversive,

01:28:07.639 --> 01:28:10.360
you have to attenuate it, ramp it down,

01:28:10.360 --> 01:28:13.039
those are the headphones or the earbuds that we talked
about before --

01:28:13.039 --> 01:28:15.210
or you teach people to

01:28:15.210 --> 01:28:19.260
mitigate the over-arousal effect of that

01:28:19.260 --> 01:28:22.830
through exercise, systematic desensitization, relaxation training.

01:28:22.830 --> 01:28:25.719
The last point here -- paired stimulating -- is pairing

01:28:25.719 --> 01:28:28.699
that aversive event with a highly reinforcing stimulus.

01:28:28.699 --> 01:28:32.519
So you'll say, "I'll put up with  the aversive to be in this."

01:28:32.519 --> 01:28:36.579
One of my older children says, "That's
what happens to guys when they go to chick

01:28:36.579 --> 01:28:39.039
flicks."

01:28:39.039 --> 01:28:43.679
They really don't want to be there at the movie, but they're gonna eat a lot of candy and popcorn to put up with, so

01:28:43.679 --> 01:28:46.599
they're like, "Ok. If that's what you have to do to put up with it,

01:28:46.599 --> 01:28:48.719
it's okay by me." He is

01:28:48.719 --> 01:28:52.179
managing pretty well with in that system, I don't know how long it's going to last him, though.

01:28:52.179 --> 01:28:55.469
But it's working for him at the moment.

01:28:55.469 --> 01:28:58.440
When you have automatic positive reinforcement, though,
which is

01:28:58.440 --> 01:29:03.260
"This is under my control." This is stereotypy.  This is the hardest nut to crack.

01:29:03.260 --> 01:29:07.610
But you have to substitute something for it.
This is the Viewmaster example.

01:29:07.610 --> 01:29:09.640
 If you substitute something that is

01:29:09.640 --> 01:29:12.530
socially appropriate and more accessible,

01:29:12.530 --> 01:29:15.119
then you have the opportunity to teach the
child,

01:29:15.119 --> 01:29:19.719
"Get this -- get your information this way, your sensory information one way,

01:29:19.719 --> 01:29:20.800
it's more acceptable,

01:29:20.800 --> 01:29:21.830
then this other way

01:29:21.830 --> 01:29:23.360
which will cause

01:29:23.360 --> 01:29:26.789
people to run away from you or to not want
to engage with you."

01:29:26.789 --> 01:29:29.840
We allow them to earn sensory reinforcement if
necessary,

01:29:29.840 --> 01:29:32.610
and we make sure that we enrich the environment.

01:29:32.610 --> 01:29:35.199
Enriching the environment is a very important
thing to do.

01:29:35.199 --> 01:29:38.880
 But in autism, sometimes environmental enrichment
means we have to think like the person

01:29:38.880 --> 01:29:40.070
with autism.

01:29:40.070 --> 01:29:41.730
 What do they like, what are they looking for?

01:29:41.730 --> 01:29:45.070
If we give them that information and make
it accessible,
 
1:29:45.070,1:29:46.559
we have a much better chance of

01:29:46.559 --> 01:29:47.490
bring together

01:29:47.490 --> 01:29:49.530
what they're looking for.

01:29:49.530 --> 01:29:53.399
Now I recognize that we're also, I think running short
on time, or running out of time, 

01:29:53.399 --> 01:29:54.980
so I am going to leave you with

01:29:54.980 --> 01:29:58.469
a little bit of a wrap up, and then if you
want to stick around we'll either talk or I'll show you a couple

01:29:58.469 --> 01:29:59.880
of videos.

01:29:59.880 --> 01:30:03.349
 The most important things I want you to take
home from this are that

01:30:03.349 --> 01:30:05.889
you can understand why behavior occurs

01:30:05.889 --> 01:30:08.969
by looking at the immediate trigger

01:30:08.969 --> 01:30:13.219
 and the setting events, the contingencies in the
environment that operate beyond that, above

01:30:13.219 --> 01:30:15.179
that at the macro level.

01:30:15.179 --> 01:30:17.639
Those could be

01:30:17.639 --> 01:30:21.349
reflective of the client's ability to predict
what's about to happen,

01:30:21.349 --> 01:30:24.170
their physical state, like hunger or illness,

01:30:24.170 --> 01:30:26.449
their ability 

01:30:26.449 --> 01:30:32.439
to experience and respond flexibly to novelty
versus things that are more highly  predictable.

01:30:32.439 --> 01:30:33.480
But at the end of the day

01:30:33.480 --> 01:30:36.889
you could begin to investigate that in some very
systematic ways.

01:30:36.889 --> 01:30:40.010
One of the systematic ways is called functional behavioral assessment

01:30:40.010 --> 01:30:41.289
and in its more 

01:30:41.289 --> 01:30:43.289
 advanced form,

01:30:43.289 --> 01:30:44.430
 functional analysis.

01:30:44.430 --> 01:30:46.659
 These allow you to know.

01:30:46.659 --> 01:30:48.760
 They allow you to generate hypotheses

01:30:48.760 --> 01:30:50.650
 would you then test.

01:30:50.650 --> 01:30:53.739
 As you think about behavior, think about
communication.

01:30:53.739 --> 01:30:54.989
 Behavior speaks.

01:30:54.989 --> 01:30:56.810
 Behavior tells you

01:30:56.810 --> 01:30:58.510
what's going on.

01:30:58.510 --> 01:31:01.070
 You then become the student,

01:31:01.070 --> 01:31:02.940
and your client because the teacher.

01:31:02.940 --> 01:31:04.900
 Because your job is to listen

01:31:04.900 --> 01:31:05.439
 and interpret.

01:31:05.439 --> 01:31:07.729
 What does that behavior mean?

01:31:07.729 --> 01:31:09.010
 In all likelihood,

01:31:09.010 --> 01:31:10.549
for a person with autism,

01:31:10.549 --> 01:31:12.070
there's a high probability

01:31:12.070 --> 01:31:13.860
 that the behavior will be consistent

01:31:13.860 --> 01:31:15.229
in its meaning.

01:31:15.229 --> 01:31:18.789
 So It generally means something that
the client understands and knows,

01:31:18.789 --> 01:31:20.550
and you can come to them.

01:31:20.550 --> 01:31:23.249
 The last point about this is:

01:31:23.249 --> 01:31:24.050
interventions

01:31:24.050 --> 01:31:26.349
 come and go, and if you've been in the field,
like

01:31:26.349 --> 01:31:29.580
a lot of people at the Child Study Center,
for a very long time,

01:31:29.580 --> 01:31:31.039
working in autism,

01:31:31.039 --> 01:31:34.449
all kinds of interventions are proposed, whether that's

01:31:34.449 --> 01:31:35.539
certain kinds of diets,

01:31:35.539 --> 01:31:39.170
 or certain kinds of activities  or hyperbaric
chambers or,

01:31:39.170 --> 01:31:43.539
you know, drinking cod liver oil is going to relieve symptoms of autism --

01:31:43.539 --> 01:31:45.159
at the end of the day,

01:31:45.159 --> 01:31:48.090
 you live and you die on the science.

01:31:48.090 --> 01:31:52.119
 If there is good science, good evidence,

01:31:52.119 --> 01:31:52.649
 that's well constructed

01:31:52.649 --> 01:31:56.149
to support certain intervention practices -- use it.

01:31:56.149 --> 01:31:57.979
 If there's not,

01:31:57.979 --> 01:31:59.630
 you may choose to use it cautiously,

01:31:59.630 --> 01:32:01.719
but you'll apply

01:32:01.719 --> 01:32:05.940
 an empirical rubric to that, you will engage in hypothesis testing

01:32:05.940 --> 01:32:08.329
and analysis in order to determine

01:32:08.329 --> 01:32:11.209
 whether what you're doing led to what you wanted

01:32:11.209 --> 01:32:12.350
to see happen,

01:32:12.350 --> 01:32:14.239
the outcome that you achieved.

01:32:14.239 --> 01:32:16.409
 The final point in this is:

01:32:16.409 --> 01:32:19.539
it's not a good intervention if it didn't
work.

01:32:19.539 --> 01:32:22.639
 You can always analyze why something doesn't
work,

01:32:22.639 --> 01:32:24.480
but if it's not effective

01:32:24.480 --> 01:32:26.949
in bringing about the outcome that you seek,

01:32:26.949 --> 01:32:31.269
it was not an effective intervention. It means
modification.

01:32:31.269 --> 01:32:34.219
You've been very patient; sorry to keep you and
run over.

01:32:34.219 --> 01:32:37.849
Any of you that want to stay, I'll show you a couple
of videos and I'm happy to have you, but have a

01:32:37.849 --> 01:32:39.679
great rest of the day and thanks for being attentive.

